# A country's cyber attacks and the learning loop: when effects operations start to pay.
scenario "A country's cyber attacks and the learning loop"
    summary "A country splits its scarce cyber operators between surveillance and attacks, and its researchers
        and analysts serve both. Surveillance pays each day that it runs, but an attack pays only when the
        country uses it, and the use burns its flaws and tools. Attacks teach the country only when it makes
        many of them, and the lessons fade. The model follows the split, the flaws, the lessons and the
        results for 25 years while AI makes attacks cheaper. It shows when military plans start to count on
        cyber attacks."
    horizon 25 years
    timescales leadership.adjust_time, 1 / defenders.calm_rate, 1 / effects_program.forget_rate
    resolution 7 days
    scope "One capable country's cyber force, its surveillance and its attacks, from 2026 for 25 years."
    library "../library"

ai_tools:  @ai_path
    "The AI tools that the country's cyber force can use. They improve over the years that the model follows."
    title "AI tools"
    role environment
    input pace: fraction = 1 in 0 to 1  @ai_pace
        "Share of AI progress that reaches the cyber force"
    input progress_time: years = 5 years in 2 years to 20 years  @ai_pace
        "Time until AI progress reaches about two thirds of its end"
    input reversal_start: years = 12 years in 1 years to 24 years  @ai_reversal
        "Time when the costs of attacks start to rise again"
    input reversal_depth: fraction = 0 in 0 to 1  @ai_reversal
        "Share of AI progress that the rise of costs takes back"
    input reversal_time: years = 3 years in 0.5 years to 10 years  @ai_reversal
        "Time until the rise of costs reaches about two thirds of its end"
    input payload_time: years = 10 years in 2 years to 30 years  @stage_paths
        "Time until AI progress on payloads reaches about two thirds of its end"
    let progress: fraction = clip(pace * (1 - exp(-t / progress_time) - reversal_depth
        * (1 - exp(-max(t - reversal_start, 0 years) / reversal_time))), 0, 1)  @ai_path
        "AI progress that reaches the cyber force's access and the running of its attacks"
    let payload_progress: fraction = clip(pace * (1 - exp(-t / payload_time) - reversal_depth
        * (1 - exp(-max(t - reversal_start, 0 years) / reversal_time))), 0, 1)  @stage_paths
        "AI progress that reaches the building of payloads, the review and the approval of an attack and
            damage assessment"

leadership:  @split_basis
    "The country's leaders, who split the country's cyber operators between surveillance and attacks each year
        from the assessed results."
    title "Leaders"
    role operator
    input experts: people = 40535 people in 5000 people to 100000 people  @experts_pool
        "Cyber experts of the country: researchers, operators and analysts"
    input share_now: fraction = 0.087 in 0.07 to 0.16  @share_today
        "Share of the experts on attacks today"
    input share_floor: fraction = 0.03 in 0.005 to 0.06  @split_limits
        "Smallest share of the operators that the leaders keep on attacks"
    input share_ceiling: fraction = 0.9 in 0.6 to 0.95  @split_limits
        "Largest share of the operators that the leaders put on attacks"
    input adjust_time: years = 3 years in 1 years to 6 years  @funding_speed
        "Time for the split to move about two thirds of the way to its target"
    input steepness: ratio = 2 in 0.5 to 10  @funding_response
        "Response of the target split to the difference in returns"
    input proportional: bool = false  @funding_forms
        "Funding moves in proportion to the difference in returns"
    input proportional_rate: per_year = 0.5 per_year in 0.1 per_year to 2 per_year  @funding_forms
        "Rate of a proportional funding response"
    input foresight: fraction = 0 in 0 to 1  @funding_rules
        "Weight that funding gives to the value of the lessons of attacks"
    input discount_rate: per_year = 0.03 per_year in 0.02 per_year to 0.1 per_year  @discount_rate
        "Discount rate of the future value of lessons"
    input assessment_interval: years = 1 years in 0.5 years to 2 years  @budget_cycle
        "Time between two assessments of the results"
    input surge_funding: USD2025_bn/yr = 0 USD2025_bn/yr in 0 USD2025_bn/yr to 2 USD2025_bn/yr  @surge_basis
        "Extra money for attacks in each year of a one-off surge"
    input surge_start: years = 0 years in 0 years to 20 years  @surge_basis
        "Time when the surge starts"
    input surge_years: years = 4 years in 1 years to 10 years  @surge_basis
        "Length of the surge"
    input expert_cost: USD2025_m/person-year = 0.42 USD2025_m/person-year in 0.25 USD2025_m/person-year to 0.6 USD2025_m/person-year  @expert_cost
        "Cost of one expert for one year"
    var attack_share: fraction = operators.share_now in 0.005 to 0.95 size 1  @split_balance
        "Share of the operators on attacks"
    var surge: "off" | "on" | "over" = "off"  @surge_basis
        "One-off surge of money for attacks"
    var assessment_error: ratio = 0 in -4 to 4 size 1  @assessment_noise
        "Error of the latest assessment of the value of attacks, in log units"
    var next_assessment: years = assessment_interval size 25 years  @budget_cycle
        "Time of the next assessment"
    let return_unit: sev/person-year = 1 sev/person-year  @sev_definition
        "Return of one expert on surveillance in 2026"
    let surge_experts: people = (surge_funding / expert_cost if surge is "on" else 0 people)  @surge_basis
        "Experts that the surge pays for"
    let attack_experts: people = attack_share * operators.active + surge_experts  @split_balance
        "Operators on attacks"
    let attack_experts_now: people = operators.attack_now  @split_balance
        "Operators on attacks today"
    let center: ratio = ln(max(operators.share_now - share_floor, 0.001)
        / max(share_ceiling - operators.share_now, 0.001))  @funding_response
        "Log-odds of today's share between the floor and the ceiling"
    let assessed_return: sev/person-year = (
        (effects_program.risk_value * exp(assessment_error + effects_program.assessment_bias)
        - effects_program.attack_cost) / attack_experts + foresight * effects_program.lesson_value
    )  @funding_rules
        "Assessed return of one operator on attacks"
    let return_gap: ratio = (
        assessed_return - surveillance_program.spying_return
    ) / surveillance_program.spying_return  @funding_rules
        "Difference between the returns of attacks and surveillance, as a share of surveillance's return"
    let target_share: fraction = (
        share_floor + (share_ceiling - share_floor) / (1 + exp(-clip(steepness * return_gap + center, -40,
        40)))
    )  @funding_response
        "Share of the experts that the leaders aim to put on attacks"
    let room: fraction = (
        clip(attack_share - share_floor, 0, 1) * clip(share_ceiling - attack_share, 0, 1)
    )  @funding_forms
        "Room of the split between its floor and its ceiling"
    let gross_return: ratio = (
        (effects_program.risk_value * exp(assessment_error + effects_program.assessment_bias) / attack_experts
        + foresight * effects_program.lesson_value) / surveillance_program.spying_return
    )  @funding_forms
        "Assessed value of one operator on attacks, before costs, as a share of surveillance's return"
    let cost_return: ratio = (
        (effects_program.attack_cost / attack_experts + surveillance_program.spying_return)
        / surveillance_program.spying_return
    )  @funding_forms
        "Costs of one operator on attacks, with the surveillance that the operator does not do, as a share of
            surveillance's return"
    flow rise: -> attack_share at (
        proportional_rate * room * gross_return if proportional else target_share / adjust_time
    )  @funding_response
        "The leaders put more of the experts on attacks."
    flow fall: attack_share -> at (
        proportional_rate * room * cost_return if proportional else attack_share / adjust_time
    )  @funding_response
        "The leaders put fewer of the experts on attacks."
    event surge_begins once when t >= surge_start and surge_funding > 0 USD2025_bn/yr:  @surge_basis
        "A one-off surge of money for attacks starts."
        surge = "on"
    event surge_ends once when since(surge_begins) >= surge_years:  @surge_basis
        "The surge of money ends."
        surge = "over"
    # The yearly assessment reads the year's results. In the central run its draw is the median, 0.
    event assess when t >= next_assessment:  @assessment_noise
        "The leaders assess the value of the year's attacks and set the next split."
        assessment_error = clip(effects_program.assessment_spread * normal(0, 1, -4, 4), -4, 4)
        next_assessment += assessment_interval

researchers:  @research_basis
    "The country's experts who find flaws that the defenders do not know and build the tools that use them.
        One group serves both missions, and its work goes to each pool of capabilities as the pool needs it."
    title "Researchers"
    role operator
    input share: fraction = 0.03 in 0.01 to 0.1  @research_share
        "Share of the experts who find flaws and build tools"
    input reserve_time: years = 1 years in 0.1 years to 5 years  @capability_reserve
        "Years of today's needs that a pool holds unused"
    input supply_time: years = 0.02 years in 0.005 years to 0.25 years  @capability_reserve
        "Time of asks below which a pool's never-used stock starts to hold back the asks"
    input gain_end: ratio = 10 in 1 to 100  @research_ai
        "Capabilities that a researcher finds in a year at the end of AI progress, as a multiple of today's"
    let people: people = share * leadership.experts  @research_share
        "Researchers"
    let need: capabilities/year = sum(Pool.research_need)  @research_split
        "Capabilities that the pools ask from the researchers in a year"
    let need_now: capabilities/year = sum(Pool.research_need_now)  @research_split
        "The same need today"
    let finds_now: capabilities/person-year = need_now / people  @research_yield
        "Capabilities that one researcher finds in a year today"
    let finds: capabilities/person-year = finds_now * exp(ai_tools.progress * ln(gain_end))  @research_ai
        "Capabilities that one researcher finds in a year"
    let output: capabilities/year = people * finds  @research_yield
        "Capabilities that the researchers find in a year"

operators:  @operator_basis
    "The country's operators. On attacks they gain access, move inside the network, prepare the effect and
        execute it. On surveillance they gain access, move inside the network and deploy implants. The leaders
        split them between the two missions, and their hours go first to the operations farthest along."
    title "Operators"
    role operator
    let attack_now: people = effects_program.attacks_now * effects_program.operator_cost_now  @expert_pools
        "Operators on attacks today"
    let spy_now: people = surveillance_program.build_share * surveillance_program.experts_now  @expert_pools
        "Operators on surveillance today: those who gain access and deploy implants"
    let people: people = attack_now + spy_now  @expert_pools
        "Operators"
    let share_now: fraction = attack_now / people  @expert_pools
        "Share of the operators on attacks today"
    input retrains: bool = false  @workforce
        "The leaders retrain operators who wait for the analysts as analysts"
    input retrain_time: years = 3 years in 0.5 years to 20 years  @workforce
        "Time for the leaders to move about two thirds of the idle operators into analysis"
    input retrain_efficiency: fraction = 0.5 in 0 to 1  @workforce
        "Analysis work of a retrained operator, as a share of an analyst's"
    var retrained_share: fraction = 0 in 0 to 0.9 size 1  @workforce
        "Share of the operators who now do analysis work"
    let retrained: people = retrained_share * people  @expert_pools
        "Operators who now do analysis work"
    let active: people = (1 - retrained_share) * people  @expert_pools
        "Operators who do the work of operations"
    let idle: people = (
        leadership.attack_experts * max(1 - effects_program.starts
        / max(effects_program.wanted_starts, 0.000001 operations/year), 0)
        + surveillance_program.spying_experts * max(1 - surveillance_program.new_accesses
        / max(surveillance_program.builds, 0.000001 accesses/year), 0)
    )  @expert_pools
        "Operators whose hours wait for the analysts to plan their operations"
    flow retrain: -> retrained_share at (idle / people / retrain_time if retrains else 0 per_year)  @workforce
        "The leaders move idle operators into analysis."

collection_specialists:  @collection_basis
    "Surveillance's collection specialists. They run collection on the deployed implants, translate, and turn
        the raw take into intelligence."
    title "Collection specialists"
    role operator
    let runners_now: people = (
        1 - surveillance_program.analysis_share
    ) * surveillance_program.experts_now - operators.spy_now  @expert_pools
        "Collection specialists who run collection today"
    let processors_now: people = (
        1 - surveillance_program.plan_share
    ) * surveillance_program.analysis_share * surveillance_program.experts_now  @expert_pools
        "Collection specialists who process the take today"
    let people: people = runners_now + processors_now  @expert_pools
        "Collection specialists"

analysts:  @analyst_basis
    "The country's analysts, who plan operations for both missions, assess the damage of attacks and use the
        intelligence. By default they go first to the mission where one more analyst-year is worth more. On
        attacks, damage assessment takes at most its share of the work waiting, and planning the rest."
    title "Analysts"
    role operator
    input load_now: fraction = 0.9 in 0.5 to 1  @analyst_load
        "Share of the analysts' capacity that today's work fills"
    input adjust_time: years = 1 years in 0.25 years to 5 years  @analyst_split
        "Time for the analysts' split to move about two thirds of the way to the missions' shares of the work"
    let people: people = effects_program.analyst_work_now + surveillance_program.planners_now  @expert_pools
        "Analysts"
    let capacity: people = (
        people + operators.retrain_efficiency * operators.retrained
    ) / load_now  @analyst_load
        "Analysis work that the analysts can do in a year, in analyst-years at today's pace"
    let share_now: fraction = effects_program.analyst_work_now / people  @analyst_split
        "Share of the analysts on attacks today"
    var attack_share: fraction = share_now in 0 to 1 size 1  @analyst_split
        "Share of the analysts on attacks"
    let asked: people = effects_program.analysis_asked + surveillance_program.analysis_asked  @analyst_split
        "Analysis work that the two missions have waiting in a year"
    input priority_steepness: ratio = 2 in 0 to 10  @analyst_split
        "Response of the analysts' priority to the ratio of the marginal values of their work"
    let attack_margin: sev/operation = (
        effects_program.risk_value / max(effects_program.attacks, 0.001 operations/year)
        / (1 + effects_program.hits / effects_program.saturation) - effects_program.sanctions
        - effects_program.burned
    )  @analyst_split
        "Value of one more attack at rest, less its costs"
    let attack_margin_value: sev/person-year = (
        max(attack_margin, 0 sev/operation) / effects_program.analysis_work
    )  @analyst_split
        "Value of one more analyst-year on attacks, where the analysts limit the attacks"
    let spy_margin_value: sev/person-year = (
        surveillance_program.spying_value * surveillance_program.returns_scale * surveillance_program.reach
        / max(surveillance_program.implants + surveillance_program.reach, 1 accesses)
        / max(surveillance_program.new_accesses * surveillance_program.plan_work, 0.001 people)
    )  @analyst_split
        "Value of one more analyst-year on surveillance's planning, where the analysts limit its new accesses"
    let priority: fraction = 1 / (1 + exp(-clip(priority_steepness
        * ln(max(attack_margin_value, 0.000000001 sev/person-year)
        / max(spy_margin_value, 0.000000001 sev/person-year)), -40, 40)))  @analyst_split
        "Priority of attacks in the analysts' staffing"
    let first_attacks: fraction = min(effects_program.analysis_need / people, 1)  @analyst_split
        "Share of the analysts on attacks when attacks get their work first, with today's slack"
    let first_spying: fraction = max(1 - surveillance_program.analysis_asked / people, 0)  @analyst_split
        "Share of the analysts on attacks when surveillance gets its work first, with today's slack"
    input by_value: bool = true  @analyst_staffing
        "The analysts go first to the mission where one more analyst-year is worth more"
    input fixed_split: bool = false  @analyst_staffing
        "The analysts' split stays at today's"
    let waiting_share: fraction = effects_program.analysis_need / max(effects_program.analysis_need
        + surveillance_program.analysis_asked, 0.001 people)  @analyst_staffing
        "Attacks' share of the analysis work that the two missions need"
    let target_share: fraction = (
        share_now if fixed_split else (priority * first_attacks + (1 - priority) * first_spying if by_value
        else waiting_share)
    )  @analyst_staffing
        "Share of the analysts that the staffing rule aims to put on attacks"
    let attack_capacity: people = attack_share * capacity  @analyst_split
        "Analysis work that the analysts on attacks can do in a year"
    let spy_capacity: people = (1 - attack_share) * capacity  @analyst_split
        "Analysis work that the analysts on surveillance can do in a year"
    flow rise: -> attack_share at target_share / adjust_time  @analyst_split
        "Analysts move to attacks where attacks' share of the work waiting is larger than their share."
    flow fall: attack_share -> at attack_share / adjust_time  @analyst_split
        "Analysts move to surveillance where it is smaller."

surveillance_program:  @surveillance_basis
    "The country's surveillance in other countries' networks. Its operators gain access with capabilities from
        the pools, move inside the networks and deploy implants. Its collection specialists run collection on
        the implants and process the take, and its analysts plan new accesses. It loses accesses to the
        defenders and to attacks."
    title "Surveillance"
    role operator
    input returns_scale: fraction = 0.8 in 0.5 to 1  @surveillance_returns
        "Exponent of the value of surveillance in the collection that its analysts process"
    input alert_sensitivity: fraction = 0.3 in 0 to 1  @surveillance_alert
        "Loss of surveillance value for each unit of defender attention or lasting capability"
    input accesses_now: accesses = 85000 accesses in 20000 accesses to 300000 accesses  @surveillance_accesses
        "Accesses that surveillance holds today"
    input used_now: fraction = 0.12 in 0.05 to 0.5  @surveillance_use
        "Share of its implants that the collection specialists run today"
    input build_share: fraction = 0.05 in 0.02 to 0.15  @surveillance_builders
        "Share of the surveillance experts who gain access and deploy implants"
    input process_time: years = 0.1 years in 0.02 years to 1 years  @collection_queue
        "Time that the take waits before the collection specialists process it"
    input analysis_share: fraction = 0.5 in 0.2 to 0.8  @surveillance_analysts
        "Share of the surveillance experts who are analysts"
    input plan_share: fraction = 0.1 in 0.02 to 0.3  @surveillance_analysts
        "Share of the analysts' work for surveillance that plans new accesses"
    input plan_target_weight: fraction = 1 in 0 to 1  @surveillance_planning_ai
        "Share of AI's help with each target of an attack that reaches the planning of a new access"
    input analysis_ai_now: fraction = 0.1 in 0 to 0.5  @analysis_ai
        "Share of surveillance's analysis work that AI does today"
    input analysis_ai_end: fraction = 0.6 in 0 to 0.9  @analysis_ai
        "Share of surveillance's analysis work that AI does at the end of AI progress"
    input own_source: fraction = 0.95 in 0.5 to 0.995  @capability_sources
        "Share of surveillance's zero-day needs today that a zero-day it already uses serves"
    input attack_source: fraction = 0.0001 in 0 to 0.0005  @capability_sources
        "Share of surveillance's zero-day needs today that a zero-day an earlier attack used serves"
    input find_share: fraction = 0.02 in 0 to 0.3  @capability_burn
        "Share of the accesses that run on a burned capability that the defenders find"
    input loss_rate: per_year = 0.5 per_year in 0.1 per_year to 2 per_year  @surveillance_loss
        "Rate at which surveillance loses accesses to the defenders, to patches and to changes"
    input run_ai_now: fraction = 0.3 in 0 to 0.9  @surveillance_ai
        "Share of the work of running accesses that automation does today"
    input run_ai_end: fraction = 0.9 in 0 to 0.95  @surveillance_ai
        "Share of the work of running accesses that AI does at the end of AI progress"
    let spying_experts: people = (1 - leadership.attack_share) * operators.active  @split_balance
        "Operators on surveillance"
    let spying_experts_now: people = operators.spy_now  @split_balance
        "Operators on surveillance today"
    let experts_now: people = (
        1 - leadership.share_now
    ) * leadership.experts - researchers.people  @expert_pools
        "Operators and analysts on surveillance today: the agency's experts who are not researchers"
    let planners_now: people = plan_share * analysis_share * experts_now  @surveillance_analysts
        "Analysts who plan surveillance's new accesses today"
    let builds_now: accesses/year = accesses_now * loss_rate  @surveillance_builds
        "Accesses that surveillance starts in a year today: those that replace its losses"
    # Today's rest point, step by step along the chain. The defenders also find some accesses through the
    # capabilities that exposed attacks burn, so surveillance holds a little fewer than its builds alone keep.
    let held_now: accesses = builds_now / (1 / effects_program.movement_time_now + loss_rate
        + access_pool.burn_loss_now)  @surveillance_builds @capability_burn
        "Accesses that surveillance has gained today and that wait for their implant"
    let deployed_now: accesses/year = held_now / effects_program.movement_time_now  @surveillance_builds
        "Implants that surveillance deploys in a year today"
    let implants_now: accesses = deployed_now / (loss_rate + access_pool.burn_loss_now
        + movement_pool.burn_loss_now)  @surveillance_builds @capability_burn
        "Implants that surveillance has deployed today"
    let build_work_now: person-years/access = spying_experts_now / (effects_program.initial_share * builds_now
        + (1 - effects_program.initial_share) * deployed_now)  @surveillance_builds
        "Operators' work to gain one access and deploy its implant today: the work that keeps today's
            accesses"
    let build_work: person-years/access = (
        build_work_now * access_stage.unit_cost / access_stage.unit_cost_now
    )  @surveillance_builds
        "Operators' work to gain one access and deploy its implant"
    let deploy_survival: fraction = 1 / (1 + (loss_rate + access_pool.burn_loss)
        * effects_program.movement_time)  @surveillance_builds
        "Share of the accesses gained that live until their implant is deployed"
    let builds: accesses/year = spying_experts / (initial_work + deploy_work
        * deploy_survival)  @surveillance_builds
        "Accesses that surveillance's operators can start in a year"
    let initial_work: person-years/access = effects_program.initial_share * build_work  @surveillance_priority
        "Operators' work to gain initial access for one access"
    let deploy_work: person-years/access = (
        1 - effects_program.initial_share
    ) * build_work  @surveillance_priority
        "Operators' work to move inside one network and deploy one implant"
    # The operators on surveillance deploy implants first, then gain new initial access with the hours left.
    let deploy_rate: accesses/year = min(accesses_held / effects_program.movement_time, spying_experts
        / deploy_work)  @surveillance_priority
        "Implants that surveillance deploys in a year"
    let access_hours: people = max(spying_experts - deploy_rate * deploy_work,
        0 people)  @surveillance_priority
        "Operators' hours on surveillance left for initial access after deployment"
    let access_rate: accesses/year = min(accesses_underway / effects_program.initial_time, access_hours
        / initial_work)  @surveillance_priority
        "Accesses where surveillance gains initial access in a year"
    let run_ai: fraction = run_ai_now + (run_ai_end - run_ai_now) * ai_tools.progress  @surveillance_ai
        "Share of the work of running accesses that AI does"
    let run_cost: ratio = 1 - run_ai + run_ai * (effects_program.supervision / effects_program.parallel
        + effects_program.compute / leadership.expert_cost)  @surveillance_ai
        "Work to run one access, as a share of the work by hand"
    let run_cost_now: ratio = 1 - run_ai_now + run_ai_now * (effects_program.supervision
        / effects_program.parallel_now + effects_program.compute_now
        / leadership.expert_cost)  @surveillance_ai
        "The same share today"
    # The collection specialists process the take first, then run collection on the implants with the hours
    # left. Today they process the take of the implants in use as it comes.
    let processed_now: accesses = used_now * implants_now  @surveillance_use
        "Take that the collection specialists process in a year today, in access-years: the implants in use"
    let reach_now: accesses = implants_now * used_now / (1 - used_now)  @surveillance_use
        "Implants that the collection specialists can run today"
    let run_work_now: people/access = collection_specialists.runners_now / reach_now  @surveillance_use
        "Collection specialists for each implant that they can run today"
    let run_work: people/access = run_work_now * run_cost / run_cost_now  @surveillance_ai
        "Collection specialists for each implant that they can run"
    let analysis_ai: fraction = (
        analysis_ai_now + (analysis_ai_end - analysis_ai_now) * ai_tools.payload_progress
    )  @analysis_ai
        "Share of surveillance's analysis work that AI does"
    let analysis_cost: ratio = 1 - analysis_ai + analysis_ai * (effects_program.supervision
        / effects_program.parallel + effects_program.compute / leadership.expert_cost)  @analysis_ai
        "Analysis work of surveillance, as a share of the work by hand"
    let analysis_cost_now: ratio = 1 - analysis_ai_now + analysis_ai_now * (effects_program.supervision
        / effects_program.parallel_now + effects_program.compute_now / leadership.expert_cost)  @analysis_ai
        "The same share today"
    let plan_work_now: person-years/access = planners_now / builds_now  @surveillance_analysts
        "Analysis work to plan one new access today"
    let plan_work: person-years/access = plan_work_now * exp(plan_target_weight
        * ln(planning_stage.target_cost / planning_stage.target_cost_now) + (1 - plan_target_weight)
        * ln(planning_stage.unit_cost / planning_stage.unit_cost_now))  @surveillance_planning_ai
        "Analysis work to plan one new access"
    let process_work_now: people/access = collection_specialists.processors_now / processed_now  @surveillance_analysts
        "Collection specialists who process one access-year of take in a year today"
    let process_work: people/access = process_work_now * analysis_cost / analysis_cost_now  @analysis_ai
        "Collection specialists who process one access-year of take in a year"
    let processed: accesses = min(collected / process_time, collection_specialists.people
        / process_work)  @collection_queue
        "Take that the collection specialists process in a year, in access-years"
    let run_hours: people = max(collection_specialists.people - processed * process_work,
        0 people)  @collection_queue
        "Collection specialists' hours left to run collection after processing"
    let reach: accesses = run_hours / run_work  @surveillance_use
        "Implants that the collection specialists can run"
    let used: accesses = implants * reach / max(implants + reach, 1 accesses)  @surveillance_use
        "Implants that the collection specialists run"
    let analysis_asked: people = builds * plan_work  @analyst_split
        "Analysis work that surveillance has waiting in a year: the planning of new accesses"
    let new_accesses: accesses/year = (
        min(builds, analysts.spy_capacity / plan_work) * access_pool.spy_served * movement_pool.spy_served
        * collection_pool.spy_served
    )  @analyst_split
        "Accesses that surveillance starts in a year: those that its operators can start and its analysts
            plan"
    let burned_accesses: accesses/year = (
        access_pool.burn_loss * (accesses_held + implants) + movement_pool.burn_loss * implants
    )  @capability_burn
        "Accesses that the defenders find in a year through the capabilities that exposed attacks burn"
    let burned_accesses_now: accesses/year = (
        access_pool.burn_loss_now * (held_now + implants_now) + movement_pool.burn_loss_now * implants_now
    )  @capability_burn
        "The same accesses today"
    let value_now: sev/year = experts_now * leadership.return_unit  @surveillance_returns
        "Value of surveillance in a year today"
    let life_value: sev/access = value_now / (accesses_now * loss_rate)  @capability_burn
        "Value of surveillance that one access gives in its life today"
    let spying_value: sev/year = (
        value_now * exp(returns_scale * ln(max(processed, 1e-6 accesses) / processed_now))
        * exp(-alert_sensitivity * (defenders.alertness - defenders.alert_now + defenders.hardening))
    )  @surveillance_returns
        "Value of surveillance in a year"
    let spying_return: sev/person-year = spying_value / spying_experts  @surveillance_returns
        "Return of one operator on surveillance"
    let spying_return_now: sev/person-year = value_now / spying_experts_now  @surveillance_returns
        "The same return today"
    # The accesses of surveillance along its chain, and the take that waits. Each starts at today's rest value.
    var accesses_underway: accesses = builds_now * effects_program.initial_time_now in 0 accesses to 1000000000 accesses size 10000 accesses  @surveillance_loop
        "Accesses where surveillance is gaining initial access"
    var accesses_held: accesses = held_now in 0 accesses to 1000000000 accesses size 10000 accesses  @surveillance_loop
        "Accesses that surveillance has gained, while it moves inside the network to deploy their implants"
    var implants: accesses = implants_now in 0 accesses to 1000000000 accesses size 10000 accesses  @surveillance_loop
        "Implants that surveillance has deployed and collects from"
    var collected: access-years = processed_now * process_time in 0 access-years to 1000000000 access-years size 10000 access-years  @collection_queue
        "Take that waits for the collection specialists, in access-years"
    flow start_access: -> accesses_underway at new_accesses  @surveillance_builds
        "Surveillance starts the accesses that its analysts plan."
    # Each step moves in parts, one for each source of the capabilities that it needs, as the steps of attacks
    # do. Initial access needs one capability of access.
    flow access_free: accesses_underway -> accesses_held at access_rate * access_pool.spy_part_free  @surveillance_loop @one_day_chance
        "Surveillance gains initial access through known flaws or capabilities that it already uses."
    flow access_kept at access_rate * access_pool.spy_part_kept:  @surveillance_loop @capability_use
        "Surveillance gains initial access with kept capabilities of earlier attacks."
        accesses_underway -> accesses_held
        access_pool.kept_attack -> access_pool.kept_shared at access_rate * access_pool.spy_part_kept * (1 capabilities/access)
    flow access_new at access_rate * access_pool.spy_part_new:  @surveillance_loop @capability_use
        "Surveillance gains initial access with capabilities that no one has used."
        accesses_underway -> accesses_held
        access_pool.fresh -> access_pool.spy_used at access_rate * access_pool.spy_part_new * (1 capabilities/access)
    share accesses_underway limit access_rate  @surveillance_loop
    # Deployment needs one capability to move inside the network and one to collect, each from its own source.
    flow deploy_free_free: accesses_held -> implants at deploy_rate * movement_pool.spy_part_free * collection_pool.spy_part_free  @surveillance_loop @one_day_chance
        "Surveillance deploys implants through known flaws or capabilities that it already uses."
    flow deploy_free_new at deploy_rate * movement_pool.spy_part_free * collection_pool.spy_part_new:  @surveillance_loop @capability_use
        "Surveillance deploys implants with a collection capability that no one has used."
        accesses_held -> implants
        collection_pool.fresh -> collection_pool.spy_used at deploy_rate * movement_pool.spy_part_free * collection_pool.spy_part_new * (1 capabilities/access)
    flow deploy_kept_free at deploy_rate * movement_pool.spy_part_kept * collection_pool.spy_part_free:  @surveillance_loop @capability_use
        "Surveillance deploys implants with kept capabilities of earlier attacks to move inside the network."
        accesses_held -> implants
        movement_pool.kept_attack -> movement_pool.kept_shared at deploy_rate * movement_pool.spy_part_kept * collection_pool.spy_part_free * (1 capabilities/access)
    flow deploy_kept_new at deploy_rate * movement_pool.spy_part_kept * collection_pool.spy_part_new:  @surveillance_loop @capability_use
        "Surveillance deploys implants with kept capabilities of earlier attacks to move, and a new collection
            capability."
        accesses_held -> implants
        movement_pool.kept_attack -> movement_pool.kept_shared at deploy_rate * movement_pool.spy_part_kept * collection_pool.spy_part_new * (1 capabilities/access)
        collection_pool.fresh -> collection_pool.spy_used at deploy_rate * movement_pool.spy_part_kept * collection_pool.spy_part_new * (1 capabilities/access)
    flow deploy_new_free at deploy_rate * movement_pool.spy_part_new * collection_pool.spy_part_free:  @surveillance_loop @capability_use
        "Surveillance deploys implants with a capability to move inside the network that no one has used."
        accesses_held -> implants
        movement_pool.fresh -> movement_pool.spy_used at deploy_rate * movement_pool.spy_part_new * collection_pool.spy_part_free * (1 capabilities/access)
    flow deploy_new_new at deploy_rate * movement_pool.spy_part_new * collection_pool.spy_part_new:  @surveillance_loop @capability_use
        "Surveillance deploys implants with new capabilities to move and to collect."
        accesses_held -> implants
        movement_pool.fresh -> movement_pool.spy_used at deploy_rate * movement_pool.spy_part_new * collection_pool.spy_part_new * (1 capabilities/access)
        collection_pool.fresh -> collection_pool.spy_used at deploy_rate * movement_pool.spy_part_new * collection_pool.spy_part_new * (1 capabilities/access)
    flow access_lost: accesses_held -> at loss_rate * accesses_held  @surveillance_loss
        "Surveillance loses accesses to the defenders, to patches and to changes."
    flow access_burned: accesses_held -> at access_pool.burn_loss * accesses_held  @capability_burn
        "The defenders find accesses through the capabilities that exposed attacks burn."
    share accesses_held limit deploy_rate + (loss_rate
        + access_pool.burn_loss) * accesses_held  @surveillance_loop
    flow implant_lost: implants -> at loss_rate * implants  @surveillance_loss
        "Surveillance loses implants to the defenders, to patches and to changes."
    flow implant_burned: implants -> at (
        access_pool.burn_loss + movement_pool.burn_loss
    ) * implants  @capability_burn
        "The defenders find implants through the capabilities that exposed attacks burn."
    share implants limit (loss_rate + access_pool.burn_loss
        + movement_pool.burn_loss) * implants  @surveillance_loop
    flow collect: -> collected at used  @collection_queue
        "The collection specialists collect the take of the implants that they run: one access-year of take
            for each implant-year."
    flow process: collected -> at processed  @collection_queue
        "The collection specialists process the take into intelligence."

defenders:  @defender_basis
    "The defenders of the target networks in other countries. Their attention follows the threat flow that
        they answer and fades within months, they fix what attacks expose, and their lasting capability can
        grow."
    title "Defenders"
    role responder
    input alert_now: ratio = 0.05 in 0 to 0.2  @alertness_today
        "Attention of the defenders today, as a loss of log-odds of an attack's success"
    input calm_rate: per_year = 2 per_year in 1 per_year to 3 per_year  @alertness_fades
        "Rate at which the attention of the defenders fades"
    input force_share: fraction = 0.05 in 0.01 to 0.3  @threat_flow
        "Share of the threat flow that the defenders answer that comes from the force's attacks today"
    input attention_cap: ratio = 6 in 2 to 20  @attention_cap
        "Most attention that the threat flow can draw, as a multiple of today's"
    input hardening_rate: per_year = 0 per_year in 0 per_year to 0.15 per_year  @hardening
        "Yearly gain of the defenders' lasting capability, as a loss of log-odds of an attack's success"
    input ai_hardening: ratio = 0 in 0 to 1.5  @defender_ai
        "Loss of log-odds of an attack's success that the defenders' AI gives at the end of AI progress"
    input discovery_now: per_year = 0.057 per_year in 0 per_year to 0.145 per_year  @loss_routes
        "Share of the force's stockpiled flaws that others find on their own and disclose in a year, today"
    input use_discovery_now: per_year = 1.08 per_year in 0.057 per_year to 2 per_year  @loss_routes
        "The same share of the flaws that the force has in use on targets, where defenders can see them at
            work"
    input patch_now: per_year = 0.044 per_year in 0 per_year to 0.1 per_year  @loss_routes
        "Share of the flaws that patches and code changes made for other reasons close in a year, today"
    input irrelevance_now: per_year = 0.044 per_year in 0 per_year to 0.1 per_year  @loss_routes
        "Share of the flaws whose systems leave service or stop mattering in a year"
    input close_gain: ratio = 2 in 0 to 5  @rediscovery
        "Growth of discovery and patching at the end of AI progress, as a multiple of today's rates"
    input within_now: fraction = 0.85 in 0.5 to 1  @outcome_correlation
        "Correlation of the outcomes on the targets of one attack today"
    input fix_rate: per_year = 1.5 per_year in 0.2 per_year to 30 per_year  @known_flaws
        "Rate at which the defenders fix a known flaw on a target"
    input residual: fraction = 0.25 in 0.05 to 0.5  @known_flaws
        "Share of targets on which a known flaw stays open"
    input common_spread: ratio = 0.3 in 0.1 to 0.46  @common_factor
        "Spread of the log-odds of a plan's success from the defenders that all of its attacks face"
    input shared_failure: fraction = 0.05 in 0 to 0.3  @shared_failure
        "Chance that a step that the attacks of a plan share fails at its start"
    input failure_rate: per_year = 0.6 per_year in 0 per_year to 3 per_year  @shared_failure
        "Rate at which the defenders find and stop a step that the attacks of a plan share"
    let threat: ratio = (
        1 - force_share + force_share * effects_program.made_targets
        / (effects_program.attacks_now * effects_program.targets_now)
    )  @threat_flow
        "Threat flow that the defenders answer, as a multiple of today's"
    # Attention saturates: it rises with the threat flow towards its cap, and today's threat draws today's.
    let attention_target: ratio = alert_now * attention_cap * threat / (threat + attention_cap
        - 1)  @attention_cap
        "Attention that the threat flow draws"
    let hardening: ratio = hardening_rate * t + ai_hardening * ai_tools.progress  @hardening
        "Loss of log-odds of an attack's success from the defenders' lasting gains"
    let closing: ratio = 1 + close_gain * ai_tools.progress  @rediscovery
        "Discovery and patching as a multiple of today's: AI helps the defenders find and close flaws"
    let rediscovery_now: per_year = discovery_now + patch_now + irrelevance_now  @loss_routes
        "Rate at which a stockpiled flaw is lost today, by every route"
    let rediscovery: per_year = (
        discovery_now + patch_now
    ) * closing + irrelevance_now  @loss_routes @rediscovery
        "Rate at which a flaw that the force holds unused is lost: found, patched or no longer relevant"
    let use_rediscovery_now: per_year = use_discovery_now + patch_now + irrelevance_now  @loss_routes
        "The same rate of a flaw in use today"
    let use_rediscovery: per_year = (
        use_discovery_now + patch_now
    ) * closing + irrelevance_now  @loss_routes @rediscovery
        "Rate at which a flaw that the force uses on targets is lost"
    # Attention is a loss of log-odds; the odds of success are clipped at 40, so it needs no higher value.
    var alertness: ratio = alert_now in 0 to 40 size 1  @alertness_balance
        "Attention of the defenders, as a loss of log-odds of an attack's success"
    flow alert: -> alertness at calm_rate * attention_target  @attention_cap
        "The threat flow draws the defenders' attention."
    flow calm: alertness -> at alertness * calm_rate  @alertness_fades
        "The attention of the defenders fades."

other_states:  @politics_basis
    "The other countries that attribute attacks and answer them with sanctions or replies."
    title "Other countries"
    role environment
    input sanction_cost: sev/operation = 10 sev/operation in 1 sev/operation to 100 sev/operation  @sanction_cost
        "Cost of the sanctions and replies that one attributed attack draws"
    input escalation: years/operation = 0 years/operation in 0 years/operation to 0.01 years/operation  @escalation
        "Growth of the cost of sanctions with the attributed attacks in a year"
    let attribution: fraction = 1 - exp(sum(Channel.miss))  @attribution_channels
        "Chance that other countries attribute an attack to the country"
    let attribution_now: fraction = 1 - exp(sum(Channel.miss_now))  @attribution_channels
        "Chance today that other countries attribute an attack to the country"

military_planners:  @planning_basis
    "The country's military planners, who build plans around cyber attacks only when a plan of attacks gives
        its intended effect reliably."
    title "Military planners"
    role operator
    input tolerance: fraction = 0.25 in 0.2 to 0.5  @plan_tolerance
        "Shortfall from the planned share of targets hit that the planners accept"
    input acceptance: fraction = 0.8 in 0.75 to 0.95  @plan_tolerance
        "Chance of meeting the plan that the planners need"
    input two_sided: bool = false  @plan_tolerance
        "A plan also fails when its attacks hit more than the tolerance above the planned share"
    input objective_share: fraction = 0.2 in 0.05 to 1  @objective_share
        "Share of the force's attacks that can serve one objective"
    input forecast_floor: ratio = 0.1 in 0.1 to 0.3  @forecast_error
        "Error of the planners' forecast of the share of targets hit that lessons do not remove"
    input passes_needed: ratio = 3 in 1 to 10  @doctrine_time
        "Planning reviews in a row that a plan must pass before plans count on cyber attacks"
    input misses_needed: ratio = 2 in 1 to 6  @doctrine_lapse
        "Planning reviews in a row that every plan must miss before plans stop counting on cyber attacks"
    input review_interval: years = 1 years in 0.5 years to 2 years  @doctrine_time
        "Time between two planning reviews"
    input doctrine_value: fraction = 0 in 0 to 1  @doctrine_feedback
        "Extra value of each effect once plans count on cyber attacks"
    input war: bool = false  @wartime
        "The country goes to war"
    input war_start: years = 0 years in 0 years to 24 years  @wartime
        "Time when the war starts"
    input war_review_interval: years = 0.25 years in 0.1 years to 1 years  @wartime
        "Time between two planning reviews in war"
    var tempo: "peace" | "war" = "peace"  @wartime
        "Tempo of the country's operations"
    var next_review: years = review_interval size 25 years  @doctrine_time
        "Time of the next planning review"
    var passes_in_a_row: ratio = 0 in 0 to 1000 size 10  @doctrine_time
        "Planning reviews in a row that a plan passes"
    var misses_in_a_row: ratio = 0 in 0 to 1000 size 10  @doctrine_lapse
        "Planning reviews in a row that every plan misses"
    var doctrine: "none" | "formed" | "lapsed" = "none"  @doctrine_time
        "Plans that count on cyber attacks"
    var formed_length: days = 0 days in 0 days to 100 days size 30 days  @plan_test
        "Length of the plan that doctrine last formed around"
    let forecast_spread: ratio = sqrt(forecast_floor ** 2 + (1 - effects_program.success)
        / effects_program.success / (effects_program.know_how / (1 targets) + 1))  @forecast_error
        "Error of the planners' forecast of the share of targets hit, as a share of it"
    let plan_error: ratio = (1 - tolerance) * forecast_spread  @forecast_error
        "Spread of the threshold that the forecast error gives a plan, as a share of the planned share"
    let best_chance: fraction = max(Plan.chance, 0)  @plan_test
        "Chance that the best plan meets its plan"
    let best_length: days = max(Plan.best_term, 0 days)  @plan_test
        "Length of the best plan"
    status ok when doctrine is "formed"
        "Plans count on cyber attacks"
    status degraded when doctrine is "lapsed"
        "Plans stopped counting on cyber attacks"
    status inactive
        "Plans do not count on cyber attacks"
    event war_begins once when war and t >= war_start:  @wartime
        "The country goes to war: moments to use its attacks come often, and the planners review every
            quarter."
        tempo = "war"
        next_review = t + war_review_interval
    event review when t >= next_review:  @doctrine_time
        met best_chance >= acceptance:
            "The planners find that a plan of cyber attacks meets its plan often enough."
            passes_in_a_row += 1
            misses_in_a_row = 0
            next_review += (war_review_interval if tempo is "war" else review_interval)
        missed else:
            "The planners find that every plan of cyber attacks misses too often."
            passes_in_a_row = 0
            misses_in_a_row += 1
            next_review += (war_review_interval if tempo is "war" else review_interval)
    event forms when not (doctrine is "formed") and passes_in_a_row >= passes_needed:  @doctrine_time
        "Military plans start to count on cyber attacks."
        doctrine = "formed"
        formed_length = best_length
    event lapses when doctrine is "formed" and misses_in_a_row >= misses_needed:  @doctrine_lapse
        "Military plans stop counting on cyber attacks."
        doctrine = "lapsed"

Plan:  @plan_test
    "A plan of one length that counts on the cyber attacks that the force can mass on one objective."
    role military_planners
    input length: days = 30 days in 1 days to 91 days  @plan_lengths
        "Length of the plan"
    let finished: operations = effects_program.ready_rate * effect_pool.attack_served * length  @held_capabilities @capability_pool
        "Attacks that the force finishes in the plan's time and that the pools can equip"
    let attacks: ratio = max(military_planners.objective_share * (effects_program.held_ready + finished)
        / (1 operations), 1)  @held_capabilities @objective_share
        "Attacks of the plan: those that the force holds ready and finishes in the plan's time, for the
            objective"
    let size: ratio = max((effects_program.held_targets + finished * effects_program.positioned_size)
        / max(effects_program.held_ready + finished, 0.001 operations) / (1 targets/operation),
        1)  @held_capabilities @objective_share
        "Targets of one attack of the plan, on average"
    let target_mass: ratio = max(military_planners.objective_share
        * (effects_program.held_targets + finished * effects_program.positioned_size) / (1 targets),
        1)  @plan_spread
        "Targets of the plan"
    let spread_mass: ratio = (
        military_planners.objective_share * (effects_program.held_spread + finished
        * effects_program.positioned_size * effects_program.made_spread) / (1 targets)
    )  @plan_spread
        "Targets of the plan, each attack's weighted by the correlation of its targets"
    let depth: fraction = effects_program.concentration_now * effects_program.types_now / ((1
        + effects_program.diversity * max(size -1, 0)) * (1 capabilities/operation))  @shared_depth
        "Share of the plan's effect that a failed shared capability takes"
    let failure: fraction = 1 - (1 - defenders.shared_failure) * exp(-defenders.failure_rate
        * length)  @shared_failure
        "Chance that one capability that today's attacks of a plan share fails in the plan's time"
    let failures: ratio = (
        (1 + effects_program.diversity * max(size -1, 0)) * (1 capabilities/operation)
        / effects_program.types_now * -ln(1 - failure)
    )  @shared_failure
        "Expected number of the plan's shared capabilities that fail: each kind of target system needs its
            own, and each fails with today's chance"
    let luck: ratio = (
        (1 - effects_program.success) / effects_program.success * max(spread_mass, target_mass) / target_mass
        ** 2
    )  @plan_spread
        "Spread of the share of targets hit from the luck of each target, squared"
    let common: ratio = (1 - effects_program.success) ** 2 * defenders.common_spread ** 2  @plan_spread
        "Spread of the share of targets hit from a cause that all targets share, squared"
    let attack_spread: ratio = sqrt(luck + common)  @plan_spread
        "Spread of the share of targets that the plan's attacks hit, as a share of its mean"
    let spread: ratio = sqrt(attack_spread ** 2 + military_planners.plan_error ** 2)  @plan_spread
        "Spread of the share of targets hit around the planned share, as a share of it"
    let held: fraction = (
        erf(military_planners.tolerance / (sqrt(2) * spread)) if military_planners.two_sided
        else (1 + erf(military_planners.tolerance / (sqrt(2) * spread))) / 2
    )  @plan_spread
        "Chance of meeting the plan when no shared capability fails"
    let loss_1: fraction = min(depth, 1)  @shared_failure
        "Share of the plan's effect that one failed shared capability takes"
    let loss_2: fraction = min(2 * depth, 1)  @shared_failure
        "Share that two failed shared capabilities take"
    let loss_3: fraction = min(3 * depth, 1)  @shared_failure
        "Share that three failed shared capabilities take"
    let none_fail: fraction = exp(-failures)  @shared_failure
        "Chance that no shared capability fails: the failures are independent, so their count is Poisson"
    let two_or_more: fraction = 1 - none_fail * (1 + failures)  @shared_failure
        "Chance that two or more shared capabilities fail"
    let three_or_more: fraction = two_or_more - none_fail * failures ** 2 / 2  @shared_failure
        "Chance that three or more fail"
    let four_or_more: fraction = three_or_more - none_fail * failures ** 3 / 6  @shared_failure
        "Chance that four or more fail"
    let tail_count: ratio = (
        clip(failures * three_or_more / four_or_more, 4, 1000) if four_or_more > 1e-12 else 4
    )  @shared_failure
        "Mean number of failed shared capabilities when four or more fail"
    let tail_spread: ratio = (
        max((failures ** 2 * two_or_more + failures * three_or_more) / four_or_more - tail_count ** 2, 0)
        if four_or_more > 1e-12 else 0
    )  @shared_failure
        "Variance of that number"
    let loss_4: fraction = clip(tail_count * depth, min(4 * depth, 1), 1)  @shared_failure
        "Share that four or more failed shared capabilities take, at their mean number"
    let spread_1: ratio = sqrt((1 - loss_1) * luck + (1 - loss_1) ** 2 * common + military_planners.plan_error
        ** 2)  @shared_failure
        "Spread of the share of targets hit when one shared capability fails: the luck of the attacks left"
    let spread_2: ratio = sqrt((1 - loss_2) * luck + (1 - loss_2) ** 2 * common + military_planners.plan_error
        ** 2)  @shared_failure
        "The same spread when two fail"
    let spread_3: ratio = sqrt((1 - loss_3) * luck + (1 - loss_3) ** 2 * common + military_planners.plan_error
        ** 2)  @shared_failure
        "The same spread when three fail"
    let spread_4: ratio = sqrt((1 - loss_4) * luck + (1 - loss_4) ** 2 * common + military_planners.plan_error
        ** 2 + (depth ** 2 * tail_spread if tail_count * depth < 1 else 0))  @shared_failure
        "The same spread when four or more fail, with the spread of their number while they take less than the
            whole plan"
    let held_1: fraction = (
        (erf((loss_1 + military_planners.tolerance) / (sqrt(2) * spread_1))
        - erf((loss_1 - military_planners.tolerance) / (sqrt(2) * spread_1))) / 2
        if military_planners.two_sided else (1 + erf((military_planners.tolerance - loss_1)
        / (sqrt(2) * spread_1))) / 2
    )  @shared_failure
        "Chance of meeting the plan when one shared capability fails"
    let held_2: fraction = (
        (erf((loss_2 + military_planners.tolerance) / (sqrt(2) * spread_2))
        - erf((loss_2 - military_planners.tolerance) / (sqrt(2) * spread_2))) / 2
        if military_planners.two_sided else (1 + erf((military_planners.tolerance - loss_2)
        / (sqrt(2) * spread_2))) / 2
    )  @shared_failure
        "Chance of meeting the plan when two fail"
    let held_3: fraction = (
        (erf((loss_3 + military_planners.tolerance) / (sqrt(2) * spread_3))
        - erf((loss_3 - military_planners.tolerance) / (sqrt(2) * spread_3))) / 2
        if military_planners.two_sided else (1 + erf((military_planners.tolerance - loss_3)
        / (sqrt(2) * spread_3))) / 2
    )  @shared_failure
        "Chance of meeting the plan when three fail"
    let held_4: fraction = (
        (erf((loss_4 + military_planners.tolerance) / (sqrt(2) * spread_4))
        - erf((loss_4 - military_planners.tolerance) / (sqrt(2) * spread_4))) / 2
        if military_planners.two_sided else (1 + erf((military_planners.tolerance - loss_4)
        / (sqrt(2) * spread_4))) / 2
    )  @shared_failure
        "Chance of meeting the plan when four or more fail"
    let chance: fraction = (
        none_fail * (held + failures * held_1 + failures ** 2 / 2 * held_2 + failures ** 3 / 6 * held_3)
        + four_or_more * held_4
    )  @plan_test
        "Chance that the plan's attacks hit their planned share of targets, within the tolerance: the mean
            over the number of shared capabilities that fail"
    let best_term: days = (length if chance >= military_planners.best_chance else 0 days)  @plan_test
        "The plan's length if it is the best plan, else zero"

day_plan: Plan
    "A plan of one day: a salvo that opens an operation."
    length = 1 days  @plan_lengths

week_plan: Plan
    "A plan of one week."
    length = 7 days  @plan_lengths

month_plan: Plan
    "A plan of one month: a campaign."

quarter_plan: Plan
    "A plan of one quarter: a long campaign."
    length = 91 days  @plan_lengths

effects_program:  @program_basis
    "The country's cyber force when it makes attacks: its experts, its access and its tools. An AI agent does
        more of the work as AI improves."
    title "Effects program"
    role adversary
    input actor = "The AI"
    input supervision: ratio = 0.2 in 0.05 to 0.5  @stage_supervision
        "Expert work to supervise AI work, for each unit of it, when one expert runs one attack at a time"
    input parallel_now: ratio = 1 in 1 to 3  @stage_supervision
        "Attacks that one expert supervises at once today"
    input parallel_end: ratio = 10 in 1 to 50  @stage_supervision
        "Attacks that one expert supervises at once at the end of AI progress"
    input compute_now: USD2025_m/person-year = 0.1 USD2025_m/person-year in 0.02 USD2025_m/person-year to 2 USD2025_m/person-year  @stage_compute
        "Cost of the compute for AI work today, per expert-year of the work that it replaces"
    input compute_end: USD2025_m/person-year = 0.01 USD2025_m/person-year in 0.001 USD2025_m/person-year to 0.4 USD2025_m/person-year  @stage_compute
        "Cost of the compute for AI work at the end of AI progress"
    input warning_time: years = 0.1 years in 0.02 years to 0.5 years  @waiting_cost
        "Time from the decision to attack until the attack must land"
    input waiting_sharpness: ratio = 2 in 1 to 4  @waiting_cost
        "Sharpness of the change from attacks built on demand to attacks built in advance"
    input access_decay: per_year = 0.3 per_year in 0.1 per_year to 1 per_year  @access_decay
        "Rate at which access built in advance is lost to patches and changes"
    input upkeep: per_year = 0.2 per_year in 0.05 per_year to 0.5 per_year  @waiting_cost
        "Work each year to keep access built in advance, as a share of its build"
    input use_rate: per_year = 0.5 per_year in 0.1 per_year to 2 per_year  @waiting_cost
        "Rate at which a moment to use an attack built in advance comes"
    input cost_learning: fraction = 0.3 in 0 to 0.6  @cost_learning
        "Share of the work of an attack that all lessons save"
    input exposure: fraction = 0.25 in 0.02 to 0.99  @exposure_basis
        "Chance that an attack exposes its access and tools when the country uses it"
    input spy_source: fraction = 0.1 in 0 to 0.5  @capability_sources
        "Share of an attack's zero-days today that surveillance already uses"
    input reuse_source: fraction = 0.05 in 0 to 0.15  @capability_sources
        "Share of an attack's zero-days today that an earlier attack used and did not burn"
    input initial_share: fraction = 0.5 in 0.1 to 0.9  @access_split
        "Share of the time and the work of access that initial access takes: movement inside the network takes
            the rest"
    input concentration_now: fraction = 0.5 in 0.2 to 0.9  @plan_concentration
        "Share of a plan's effect today that depends on one capability that its attacks share"
    input diversity: fraction = 0.1 in 0 to 1  @target_kinds
        "Chance that a further target of an attack runs a kind of system that its other targets do not"
    input tailoring_end: fraction = 0.8 in 0 to 1  @tailoring
        "Share of the steps that the targets of an attack share that AI tailors to each target at the end"
    input targets_now: targets/operation = 1 targets/operation in 1 targets/operation to 3 targets/operation  @targets_basis
        "Targets of one attack today"
    input targets_end: targets/operation = 30 targets/operation in 1 targets/operation to 100 targets/operation  @targets_basis
        "Targets of one attack at the end of AI progress"
    input success_now: fraction = 0.2 in 0.05 to 0.4  @success_basis
        "Chance today that an attack gives its intended effect on one target"
    input success_best: fraction = 0.7 in 0.4 to 0.95  @success_basis
        "Chance with every lesson learned"
    input value_scale: ratio = 1 in 0.25 to 4  @value_scale
        "Value of an effect as a multiple of the value that today's split shows"
    input saturation: targets/year = 1000 targets/year in 100 targets/year to 10000 targets/year  @diminishing_value
        "Targets hit in a year at which each effect is worth half as much"
    input value_learning: ratio = 1 in 0 to 3  @value_learning
        "Extra value of each effect with every lesson learned"
    let value_learning_log: ratio = ln(1 + value_learning)  @value_learning
        "Logarithm of the growth of the value of each effect with every lesson learned"
    input risk_aversion: ratio = 0 in 0 to 4  @risk_value
        "Aversion of the leaders to the spread of the yearly value of attacks"
    input assessment_reliability: fraction = 0.4 in 0.2 to 0.6  @assessment_basis
        "Share of what one outcome teaches that the damage assessment keeps"
    input assessment_noise: ratio = 0.8 in 0.3 to 2  @assessment_noise
        "Error of the damage assessment of one outcome, in log units"
    input common_error: ratio = 0.3 in 0 to 1  @assessment_common
        "Error of a yearly assessment that all of its outcomes share, in log units"
    input assessment_bias_change: ratio = 0 in -0.5 to 0.5  @assessment_bias
        "Change of the bias of the assessment at the end of AI progress, in log units"
    input access_learning: fraction = 0.1 in 0 to 0.3  @access_lessons
        "Share of the work of access that each doubling of the lessons of access saves"
    input access_forget_rate: per_year = 0.2 per_year in 0.05 per_year to 0.5 per_year  @access_lessons
        "Rate at which the force loses its lessons of access"
    input task_overlap: fraction = 0.7 in 0.3 to 1  @access_lessons
        "Share of surveillance's work to build an access that teaches the access of an attack"
    input war_use_rate: per_year = 12 per_year in 2 per_year to 52 per_year  @wartime
        "Rate at which the moment to use an attack held ready comes in war"
    input war_warning_time: years = 0.02 years in 0.005 years to 0.1 years  @wartime
        "Time from the decision to attack until the attack must land, in war"
    input prior_strength: targets = 5000 targets in 300 targets to 20000 targets  @learning_curve
        "Assessed outcomes at which lessons have half of the weight in the force's choices"
    input curve_shape: ratio = 1 in 1 to 3  @learning_curve
        "Shape of the learning curve: 1 for the Bayesian weight of the evidence"
    input exponential_curve: bool = false  @learning_forms
        "Lessons follow an exponential curve"
    input forget_rate: per_year = 0.3 per_year in 0.1 per_year to 0.7 per_year  @forgetting
        "Rate at which the force loses its lessons"
    input burn_share: fraction = 0 in 0 to 0.8  @burned_lessons
        "Share of today's loss of lessons that comes from exposed attacks"
    let parallel: ratio = parallel_now * exp(ai_tools.progress
        * ln(parallel_end / parallel_now))  @stage_supervision
        "Attacks that one expert supervises at once"
    let compute: USD2025_m/person-year = compute_now * exp(ai_tools.progress
        * ln(compute_end / compute_now))  @stage_compute
        "Cost of the compute for AI work, per expert-year of the work that it replaces"
    let automation: fraction = sum(Stage.ai_work) / sum(Stage.hand_work)  @stage_ai
        "Share of the work of an attack that AI does"
    let lead_time: years = sum(Stage.lead_term)  @lead_time
        "Time to build the access and the effect of one attack"
    let lead_now: years = sum(Stage.lead_term_now)  @lead_time
        "The same time today"
    let cost_now: person-years/operation = sum(Stage.cost_now)  @cost_today
        "Expert work for one attack today"
    let operator_cost_now: person-years/operation = sum(Stage.operator_cost_now)  @expert_pools
        "Operators' work for one attack today"
    let compute_spending: USD2025_bn/yr = starts * sum(Stage.ai_work) * compute  @stage_compute
        "Spending on the compute of AI work in a year"
    let attack_targets: targets/operation = targets_now * exp(ai_tools.progress
        * ln(targets_end / targets_now))  @targets_basis
        "Targets of one attack"
    let initial_time: years = initial_share * access_stage.time  @access_split
        "Time to gain initial access to a target"
    let initial_time_now: years = initial_share * access_stage.time_now  @access_split
        "The same time today"
    let movement_time: years = (1 - initial_share) * access_stage.time  @access_split
        "Time to move inside a target's network to the systems that the effect needs"
    let movement_time_now: years = (1 - initial_share) * access_stage.time_now  @access_split
        "The same time today"
    let initial_work: person-years/target = initial_share * access_stage.learned_cost / attack_targets  @access_split
        "Operators' work to gain initial access to one target"
    let movement_work: person-years/target = (
        1 - initial_share
    ) * access_stage.learned_cost / attack_targets  @access_split
        "Operators' work to move inside one target's network"
    let built_ahead: fraction = 1 - 1 / (1 + exp(waiting_sharpness * ln(lead_time / warning)))  @waiting_cost
        "Share of attacks that need access built in advance"
    let built_ahead_now: fraction = 1 - 1 / (1 + exp(waiting_sharpness
        * ln(lead_now / warning_time)))  @waiting_cost
        "Share of attacks today that need access built in advance"
    let moment_rate: per_year = (war_use_rate if military_planners.tempo is "war" else use_rate)  @wartime
        "Rate at which the moment to use an attack held ready comes"
    let warning: years = (war_warning_time if military_planners.tempo is "war" else warning_time)  @wartime
        "Time from the decision to attack until the attack must land"
    let waiting: ratio = 1 + built_ahead * (access_decay + upkeep) / moment_rate  @waiting_cost
        "Work of an attack with the cost of waiting, as a multiple of its build"
    let waiting_now: ratio = 1 + built_ahead_now * (access_decay + upkeep) / use_rate  @waiting_cost
        "The same multiple today"
    let base_cost: person-years/operation = sum(Stage.cost)  @stage_cost
        "Expert work for one attack, before lessons"
    let attacks_now: operations/year = leadership.share_now * leadership.experts / cost_now  @attack_count
        "Attacks in a year today: today's experts on attacks over the work of one attack"
    let analyst_work_now: people = attacks_now * sum(Stage.analyst_cost_now)  @expert_pools
        "Analysts on attacks today"
    let target_count: ratio = attack_targets / (1 targets/operation)  @targets_basis
        "Targets of one attack, as a number"
    let types: capabilities/operation = (
        1 + diversity * max(target_count - 1, 0)
    ) * (1 capabilities/operation)  @target_kinds
        "Kinds of target system in one attack: each needs its own capability at a step that no known flaw
            serves"
    let types_now: capabilities/operation = (
        1 + diversity * max(targets_now / (1 targets/operation) - 1, 0)
    ) * (1 capabilities/operation)  @target_kinds
        "The same kinds today"
    let shared_chance_now: fraction = success_now / (success_now + defenders.within_now
        * (1 - success_now))  @outcome_correlation
        "Chance today that the steps that the targets of an attack share work"
    let shared_part_now: ratio = ln(shared_chance_now) / ln(success_now)  @outcome_correlation
        "Share of the log-chance of success today that lies in the steps that the targets of an attack share"
    let tailoring: fraction = tailoring_end * ai_tools.progress  @tailoring
        "Share of those steps that AI tailors to each target"
    let shared_chance: fraction = exp((1 - tailoring) * shared_part_now
        * ln(success_now))  @outcome_correlation
        "Chance that the steps that the targets of an attack share work, at today's chance of success"
    let within_correlation: fraction = (
        success_now * (1 - shared_chance) / (shared_chance * (1 - success_now))
    )  @outcome_correlation
        "Correlation of the outcomes on the targets of one attack"
    let target_spread: ratio = 1 + (target_count - 1) * within_correlation  @outcome_correlation
        "Growth of the spread of one attack's outcomes from the correlation of its targets"
    let independent_outcomes: targets/operation = attack_targets / target_spread  @outcome_correlation
        "Independent outcomes of one attack"
    let lessons_now: targets = (
        assessment_reliability * attacks_now * targets_now / (1 + (targets_now / (1 targets/operation) - 1)
        * defenders.within_now) / forget_rate
    )  @lessons_today
        "Lessons that the force keeps today"
    let weight_ratio: ratio = know_how / prior_strength  @learning_curve
        "Lessons as a multiple of the prior strength"
    let ratio_now: ratio = lessons_now / prior_strength  @learning_curve
        "The same multiple today"
    let curve_power: ratio = exp(curve_shape * ln(weight_ratio))  @learning_curve
        "Lessons over the prior strength, to the power of the curve's shape"
    let curve_power_now: ratio = exp(curve_shape * ln(ratio_now))  @learning_curve
        "The same power today"
    let weight: fraction = (
        1 - exp(-weight_ratio * ln_two) if exponential_curve else 1 - 1 / (1 + curve_power)
    )  @learning_forms
        "Weight of lessons in the force's choices"
    let ln_two: ratio = ln(2)  @learning_forms
        "Natural logarithm of 2"
    # The gain is the weight's change since today over what was left to learn today: (k - k0) / (1 - k0).
    # Each form writes it without the division, so the domain proof holds for every input.
    let gain: ratio = (
        1 - exp(clip((ratio_now - weight_ratio) * ln_two, -700, 700)) if exponential_curve else 1
        - (1 + curve_power_now) / (1 + curve_power)
    )  @learning_curve
        "Gain of the lessons since today, as a share of what was left to learn"
    let cost: person-years/operation = sum(Stage.learned_cost)  @cost_learning
        "Expert work for one attack"
    let operator_cost: person-years/operation = sum(Stage.operator_cost)  @expert_pools
        "Operators' work for one attack"
    let effects_base: person-years/operation = sum(Stage.effects_cost)  @cost_learning
        "Operators' work of an attack in the stages that only attacks do, before lessons"
    let effects_share_now: fraction = sum(Stage.effects_cost_now) / operator_cost_now  @cost_learning
        "Share of today's work of the operators for an attack in the stages that only attacks do"
    let wanted_starts: operations/year = leadership.attack_experts / operator_cost  @attack_count
        "Attacks that the operators on attacks can start in a year"
    # The analysts on attacks assess the damage of the attacks made first, then plan new ones.
    let assessment_part: fraction = (
        assessing / assessment_stage.time * assessment_stage.learned_cost
        / max(assessing / assessment_stage.time * assessment_stage.learned_cost + wanted_starts
        * planning_stage.learned_cost, 0.001 people)
    )  @analyst_split
        "Share of the analysts' hours on attacks that damage assessment may take: its share of the work
            waiting"
    let assessed_rate: operations/year = min(assessing / assessment_stage.time, analysts.attack_capacity
        * assessment_part / assessment_stage.learned_cost)  @assessment_time @analyst_split
        "Attacks whose damage assessment ends in a year"
    let planning_left: people = max(analysts.attack_capacity - assessed_rate * assessment_stage.learned_cost,
        0 people)  @analyst_split
        "Analysis work on attacks that is left for planning after damage assessment"
    let supply: fraction = access_pool.attack_served * movement_pool.attack_served * effect_pool.attack_served  @capability_pool
        "Share of the attacks that the pools can equip now"
    let starts: operations/year = min(wanted_starts, planning_left
        / planning_stage.learned_cost) * supply  @attack_count
        "Attacks that the force starts in a year: those that its operators can start and its analysts plan"
    let analysis_asked: people = (
        assessing / assessment_stage.time * assessment_stage.learned_cost + wanted_starts
        * planning_stage.learned_cost
    )  @analyst_split
        "Analysis work that attacks have waiting in a year: damage assessment and the planning of new attacks"
    let analysis_need: people = (
        max(assessing / assessment_stage.time, wanted_starts) * assessment_stage.learned_cost + wanted_starts
        * planning_stage.learned_cost
    )  @analyst_split
        "Analysis work that the attacks the operators can start need in a year, or the queue if it is larger"
    let analysis_done: people = (
        assessed_rate * assessment_stage.learned_cost + starts * planning_stage.learned_cost
    )  @analyst_split
        "Analysis work on attacks that the analysts do in a year"
    # The operators' hours go to the attacks farthest along first: execution, then the effect, then access.
    let execution_asked: operations/year = (
        moment_rate * held_ready + (1 - built_ahead) * positioned_attacks / payload_stage.time
    )  @operator_priority
        "Attacks that wait for execution in a year: those held ready whose moment comes, and those built on
            demand"
    let executed_size: targets/operation = max((moment_rate * held_targets + (1 - built_ahead) * positioned
        / payload_stage.time) / max(execution_asked, 0.001 operations/year),
        1 targets/operation)  @operator_priority
        "Targets of one attack that waits for execution, on average: each keeps the targets that it was built
            with"
    let execution_work: person-years/operation = (
        execution_stage.work * execution_stage.unit_cost + execution_stage.target_work * executed_size
        * execution_stage.target_cost
    ) * execution_stage.learning  @operator_priority
        "Operators' work to execute one attack that waits for execution, after lessons"
    let execution_served: fraction = min(1, leadership.attack_experts
        / max(execution_asked * execution_work, 0.001 people))  @operator_priority
        "Share of those attacks that the operators' hours let the force execute"
    let effect_hours: people = max(leadership.attack_experts - execution_served * execution_asked
        * execution_work, 0 people)  @operator_priority
        "Operators' hours left for the effect after execution"
    let ready_rate: operations/year = min(positioned_attacks / payload_stage.time, effect_hours
        / effect_work)  @attack_pipeline @operator_priority
        "Attacks whose effect the force finishes in a year: each takes the targets of one attack"
    let movement_hours: people = max(effect_hours - (built_ahead + (1 - built_ahead) * execution_served)
        * ready_rate * effect_work, 0 people)  @operator_priority
        "Operators' hours left for movement inside the network after the effect"
    let movement_rate: targets/year = min(accesses / movement_time, movement_hours
        / movement_work)  @attack_pipeline @operator_priority
        "Targets where the force finishes its movement inside the network in a year"
    let access_hours: people = max(movement_hours - movement_rate * movement_work,
        0 people)  @operator_priority
        "Operators' hours left for initial access after movement"
    let access_rate: targets/year = min(in_access / initial_time, access_hours
        / initial_work)  @attack_pipeline @operator_priority
        "Targets where the force gains initial access in a year"
    let hold_rate: operations/year = built_ahead * ready_rate  @held_capabilities
        "Attacks built in advance in a year, which wait ready for their moment"
    let now_rate: operations/year = (
        1 - built_ahead
    ) * ready_rate * execution_served  @attack_pipeline @operator_priority
        "Attacks built on demand in a year, which the force makes as soon as they are ready"
    let attacks: operations/year = (
        now_rate * effect_pool.attack_served + moment_rate * held_ready * execution_served
    )  @attack_pipeline
        "Attacks that the force makes in a year"
    let in_access_size: targets/operation = max(in_access / max(in_access_attacks, 0.001 operations),
        1 targets/operation)  @attack_pipeline
        "Targets of one attack in initial access, on average"
    let access_size: targets/operation = max(accesses / max(access_attacks, 0.001 operations),
        1 targets/operation)  @attack_pipeline
        "Targets of one attack in movement, on average"
    let positioned_size: targets/operation = max(positioned / max(positioned_attacks, 0.001 operations),
        1 targets/operation)  @attack_pipeline
        "Targets of one attack whose effect the force builds, on average"
    let positioned_types: capabilities/operation = (
        1 + diversity * max(positioned_size / (1 targets/operation) - 1, 0)
    ) * (1 capabilities/operation)  @target_kinds
        "Kinds of target system of one attack whose effect the force builds"
    let access_target_need: capabilities/target = (
        1 + diversity * max(in_access_size / (1 targets/operation) - 1, 0)
    ) * (1 capabilities/operation) / in_access_size  @capability_use
        "Capabilities that one target in initial access needs: one for each kind of system of its attack"
    let movement_target_need: capabilities/target = (
        1 + diversity * max(access_size / (1 targets/operation) - 1, 0)
    ) * (1 capabilities/operation) / access_size  @capability_use
        "Capabilities that one target in movement needs: one for each kind of system of its attack"
    let effect_work: person-years/operation = (
        payload_stage.work * payload_stage.unit_cost + payload_stage.target_work * positioned_size
        * payload_stage.target_cost
    ) * waiting * payload_stage.learning  @operator_priority
        "Operators' work to build the effect of one attack whose effect the force builds"
    let made_spread: ratio = 1 + (positioned_size / (1 targets/operation)
        - 1) * within_correlation  @outcome_correlation
        "Growth of the spread of the outcomes of an attack whose effect the force finishes now"
    let held_size: targets/operation = max(held_targets / max(held_ready, 0.001 operations),
        1 targets/operation)  @held_capabilities
        "Targets of one attack held ready, on average"
    let assessed_size: targets/operation = max(assessing_targets / max(assessing, 0.001 operations),
        1 targets/operation)  @assessment_time
        "Targets of one attack under assessment, on average"
    let made_targets: targets/year = (
        now_rate * effect_pool.attack_served * positioned_size + moment_rate * held_targets * execution_served
    )  @attack_pipeline
        "Targets of the attacks that the force makes in a year"
    let odds_now: ratio = ln(success_now / (1 - success_now))  @success_basis
        "Log-odds of success today"
    let odds_best: ratio = ln(success_best / (1 - success_best))  @success_basis
        "Log-odds of success with every lesson learned"
    let odds: ratio = (
        odds_now + (odds_best - odds_now) * gain - (defenders.alertness - defenders.alert_now)
        - defenders.hardening
    )  @success_basis
        "Log-odds of success on one target"
    let success: fraction = 1 / (1 + exp(-clip(odds, -40, 40)))  @success_basis
        "Chance that an attack gives its intended effect on one target"
    let hits: targets/year = made_targets * success  @value_basis
        "Targets hit in a year"
    let doctrine_factor: ratio = 1 + military_planners.doctrine_value * (1 if military_planners.doctrine
        is "formed" else 0)  @doctrine_feedback
        "Growth of the value of effects once plans count on them"
    # The calibration solves today's equal returns for the value of one effect. With foresight, the
    # assessed return counts the value of lessons, which is linear in the value of one effect, so the
    # solution stays in closed form.
    let effect_value_now: sev/target = (
        (leadership.attack_experts_now * surveillance_program.spying_return_now + attack_cost_now
        + leadership.foresight * cost_slope_now * lesson_weight_now)
        / (hits_now / (1 + hits_now / saturation) * risk_factor_now + leadership.foresight * value_growth_now
        * lesson_weight_now)
    )  @revealed_split
        "Value of one intended effect that today's split shows"
    let gain_slope_now: per_target = (
        ln_two / prior_strength if exponential_curve else curve_shape * curve_power_now / ratio_now
        / (1 + curve_power_now) / prior_strength
    )  @lesson_value
        "Growth of the gain with one more lesson today"
    let lesson_weight_now: ratio = (
        lessons_now * forget_rate * gain_slope_now / (leadership.discount_rate + forget_rate)
    )  @lesson_value
        "Discounted gain of the lessons that today's attacks teach in one year"
    let value_growth_now: targets/year = (
        (hits_now * (cost_learning * effects_share_now + (1 - success_now) * (odds_best - odds_now))
        / (1 + hits_now / saturation) + hits_now * value_learning_log) / (1 + hits_now / saturation)
    )  @lesson_value
        "Growth of today's counted hits in a year with the gain"
    let cost_slope_now: sev/year = (
        attacks_now * cost_learning * effects_share_now * (other_states.attribution_now
        * other_states.sanction_cost * (1 + 2 * other_states.escalation * attacks_now
        * other_states.attribution_now) + burned_now)
    )  @lesson_value
        "Growth of today's yearly costs of attacks with the gain"
    let hits_now: targets/year = attacks_now * targets_now * success_now  @value_basis
        "Targets hit in a year today"
    let attack_cost_now: sev/year = attacks_now * (other_states.attribution_now * other_states.sanction_cost
        * (1 + other_states.escalation * attacks_now * other_states.attribution_now)
        + burned_now)  @politics_basis
        "Value lost to sanctions and burned access in a year today"
    let attack_value: sev/year = (
        value_scale * effect_value_now * hits / (1 + hits / saturation)
        * exp(clip(value_learning_log * gain, -700, 700)) * doctrine_factor
    )  @value_basis
        "Value of the attacks in a year"
    let yearly_targets: ratio = made_targets * (1 years) / (1 targets)  @value_basis
        "Targets of the attacks of one year, as a number"
    let learning_spread: fraction = (
        success * (1 - success) / (know_how / (1 targets) + prior_strength / (1 targets) + 1)
    )  @risk_value
        "Uncertainty of the chance of success, as a variance"
    let value_spread: ratio = (
        (1 - success) / success * target_spread / (yearly_targets + 1) + (1 - success) ** 2
        * defenders.common_spread ** 2 + learning_spread / success ** 2
    )  @risk_value
        "Relative variance of the value of the attacks of one year"
    let risk_factor: ratio = 1 / (1 + risk_aversion / 2 * value_spread)  @risk_value
        "Share of the value that the leaders count after the spread"
    let risk_factor_now: ratio = 1 / (1 + risk_aversion / 2
        * ((1 - success_now) / success_now * (1 + (targets_now / (1 targets/operation) - 1)
        * defenders.within_now) / (attacks_now * targets_now * (1 years) / (1 targets) + 1)
        + (1 - success_now) ** 2 * defenders.common_spread ** 2 + (1 - success_now) / success_now
        / (lessons_now / (1 targets) + prior_strength / (1 targets) + 1)))  @risk_value
        "Share of the value that the leaders count today"
    let risk_value: sev/year = attack_value * risk_factor  @risk_value
        "Value of the attacks in a year that the leaders count"
    let sanctions: sev/operation = (
        other_states.attribution * other_states.sanction_cost
        * (1 + other_states.escalation * attacks * other_states.attribution)
    )  @politics_basis
        "Cost of the sanctions and replies for one attack"
    let burned: sev/operation = (
        surveillance_program.burned_accesses * surveillance_program.life_value
        / max(attacks, 0.001 operations/year)
    )  @capability_burn
        "Surveillance value that one attack burns: the accesses that the defenders find through the
            capabilities that it burns"
    let burned_now: sev/operation = (
        surveillance_program.burned_accesses_now * surveillance_program.life_value / attacks_now
    )  @capability_burn
        "Surveillance value that one attack burns today"
    let attack_cost: sev/year = attacks * (sanctions + burned)  @politics_basis
        "Value lost to sanctions and burned access in a year"
    let forgetting: per_year = forget_rate * (1 - burn_share + burn_share * attacks
        / attacks_now)  @burned_lessons
        "Rate at which the force loses its lessons"
    let learn_rate: targets/year = assessment_reliability * assessed_rate * assessing_outcomes / max(assessing,
        0.001 operations)  @learning_basis
        "Lessons that the force gains in a year"
    let loss_rate: targets/year = know_how * forgetting  @forgetting
        "Lessons that the force loses in a year"
    # The value of what attacks teach: the change of the yearly net value with one more lesson, held for as
    # long as lessons last and discounted, for each expert on attacks.
    let gain_slope: per_target = (
        ln_two * exp(clip((ratio_now - weight_ratio) * ln_two, -700, 700)) / prior_strength
        if exponential_curve else ((1 + curve_power_now) * curve_shape * curve_power / weight_ratio
        / (1 + curve_power) ** 2 / prior_strength)
    )  @lesson_value
        "Growth of the gain with one more lesson"
    # Where the operators limit the attacks, a lesson that cuts their work lets them start more attacks; where
    # the analysts limit them, a lesson that cuts the analysts' work of planning and assessment lets them plan
    # more. The slope blends the two by the share of the attacks wanted that start. The exact slope has a kink
    # where the two limits meet, and a jump there makes the funding rule chatter, so the blend is continuous.
    let analysis_work: person-years/operation = (
        planning_stage.learned_cost + assessment_stage.learned_cost
    )  @lesson_value
        "Analysts' work to plan and assess one attack"
    let crowding: ratio = min(starts / max(wanted_starts, 0.000001 operations/year), 1)  @lesson_value
        "Share of the attacks that the operators can start that the analysts plan"
    # The slope counts the attacks that a lower cost lets the force start, which it makes once they leave the
    # pipeline: the value of a lesson is a value at rest.
    let attack_slope: operations/year = (
        wanted_starts * cost_learning * effects_base / operator_cost * crowding + (1 - crowding) * starts
        * cost_learning * (planning_stage.cost + assessment_stage.cost) / analysis_work
    )  @lesson_value
        "Growth of the attacks in a year with the gain"
    let success_slope: fraction = success * (1 - success) * (odds_best - odds_now)  @lesson_value
        "Growth of the chance of success with the gain"
    let hits_slope: targets/year = attack_targets * (success * attack_slope + attacks
        * success_slope)  @lesson_value
        "Growth of the targets hit in a year with the gain"
    let value_slope: sev/year = (
        value_scale * effect_value_now * doctrine_factor * exp(clip(value_learning_log * gain, -700, 700))
        * (hits_slope / (1 + hits / saturation) + hits * value_learning_log) / (1 + hits / saturation)
    )  @lesson_value
        "Growth of the value of the attacks of a year with the gain"
    let cost_slope: sev/year = attack_slope * (other_states.attribution * other_states.sanction_cost
        * (1 + 2 * other_states.escalation * attacks * other_states.attribution) + burned)  @lesson_value
        "Growth of the yearly costs of attacks with the gain"
    let lesson_value: sev/person-year = (
        (value_slope - cost_slope) * gain_slope / (leadership.discount_rate + forgetting)
        * assessment_reliability * independent_outcomes * crowding / operator_cost
    )  @lesson_value
        "Value of the lessons of one operator's year on attacks"
    let assessment_spread: ratio = sqrt((1 - success) / success * target_spread / (yearly_targets + 1)
        + assessment_noise ** 2 / (learn_rate * (1 years) / (1 targets) + 1) + common_error
        ** 2)  @assessment_noise
        "Spread of the error of a yearly assessment, in log units"
    let assessment_bias: ratio = assessment_bias_change * ai_tools.progress  @assessment_bias
        "Bias of the assessment of the value of attacks, in log units"
    var know_how: targets = lessons_now in 1 targets to 1000000 targets size 10000 targets  @learning_balance
        "Lessons that the force keeps: assessed outcomes"
    var attacks_made: operations = 0 operations size 100000 operations  @attack_count
        "Attacks made since the start"
    # The targets and the attacks on their way from start to assessment. Each stock starts at its rest value
    # today: what passes in a year today times the time in the stock.
    var in_access: targets = attacks_now * targets_now * initial_time_now in 0 targets to 100000000 targets size 100 targets  @attack_pipeline
        "Targets where the force is gaining initial access"
    var accesses: targets = attacks_now * targets_now * movement_time_now in 0 targets to 100000000 targets size 100 targets  @attack_pipeline
        "Targets where the force has access and moves inside the network"
    var positioned: targets = attacks_now * targets_now * payload_stage.time_now in 0 targets to 100000000 targets size 100 targets  @attack_pipeline
        "Targets where the force sits on the systems that the effect needs, while it builds the effect"
    var held_ready: operations = built_ahead_now * attacks_now / use_rate in 0 operations to 10000000 operations size 1000 operations  @held_capabilities
        "Attacks built in advance that the force holds ready for their moment"
    var assessing: operations = attacks_now * assessment_stage.time_now in 0 operations to 10000000 operations size 100 operations  @assessment_time
        "Attacks made whose damage the force is assessing"
    var in_access_attacks: operations = attacks_now * initial_time_now in 0 operations to 100000000 operations size 100 operations  @attack_pipeline
        "Attacks whose targets the force is gaining initial access to"
    var access_attacks: operations = attacks_now * movement_time_now in 0 operations to 100000000 operations size 100 operations  @attack_pipeline
        "Attacks whose targets the force has access to and moves inside"
    var positioned_attacks: operations = attacks_now * payload_stage.time_now in 0 operations to 100000000 operations size 100 operations  @attack_pipeline
        "Attacks whose effect the force builds"
    var held_spread: targets = built_ahead_now * attacks_now * targets_now * (1
        + (targets_now / (1 targets/operation) - 1)
        * defenders.within_now) / use_rate in 0 targets to 1000000000 targets size 1000 targets  @held_capabilities @outcome_correlation
        "Targets of the attacks held ready, each attack's weighted by the correlation of its targets"
    var held_outcomes: targets = built_ahead_now * attacks_now * targets_now / (1
        + (targets_now / (1 targets/operation) - 1)
        * defenders.within_now) / use_rate in 0 targets to 1000000000 targets size 1000 targets  @held_capabilities @outcome_correlation
        "Independent outcomes of the attacks held ready"
    var assessing_outcomes: targets = attacks_now * targets_now / (1
        + (targets_now / (1 targets/operation) - 1)
        * defenders.within_now) * assessment_stage.time_now in 0 targets to 1000000000 targets size 100 targets  @assessment_time @outcome_correlation
        "Independent outcomes of the attacks whose damage the force is assessing"
    var held_targets: targets = built_ahead_now * attacks_now * targets_now / use_rate in 0 targets to 1000000000 targets size 1000 targets  @held_capabilities
        "Targets of the attacks held ready: each keeps the targets that it was built with"
    var assessing_targets: targets = attacks_now * targets_now * assessment_stage.time_now in 0 targets to 1000000000 targets size 100 targets  @assessment_time
        "Targets of the attacks whose damage the force is assessing"
    flow start_build at starts * attack_targets:  @attack_count
        "The force starts attacks: their targets enter initial access."
        -> in_access
        -> in_access_attacks at starts
    # Each step moves in parts, one for each source of the capabilities that its targets need. A part moves
    # together with the stock that it draws on, so an empty source holds back only its own part.
    flow access_known at access_rate * access_pool.one_day:  @attack_pipeline @one_day_chance
        "The force gains initial access to targets through known flaws."
        in_access -> accesses
        in_access_attacks -> access_attacks at access_rate * access_pool.one_day / in_access_size
    flow access_spy at access_rate * access_pool.attack_part_spy:  @attack_pipeline @capability_use
        "The force gains initial access to targets with capabilities that surveillance uses."
        in_access -> accesses
        in_access_attacks -> access_attacks at access_rate * access_pool.attack_part_spy / in_access_size
        access_pool.spy_used -> access_pool.held_shared at access_rate * access_pool.attack_part_spy * access_target_need
    flow access_kept_shared at access_rate * access_pool.attack_part_kept_shared:  @attack_pipeline @capability_use
        "The force gains initial access to targets with kept capabilities of earlier attacks that surveillance
            also uses."
        in_access -> accesses
        in_access_attacks -> access_attacks at access_rate * access_pool.attack_part_kept_shared / in_access_size
        access_pool.kept_shared -> access_pool.held_shared at access_rate * access_pool.attack_part_kept_shared * access_target_need
    flow access_kept_attack at access_rate * access_pool.attack_part_kept_attack:  @attack_pipeline @capability_use
        "The force gains initial access to targets with kept capabilities of earlier attacks."
        in_access -> accesses
        in_access_attacks -> access_attacks at access_rate * access_pool.attack_part_kept_attack / in_access_size
        access_pool.kept_attack -> access_pool.held_attack at access_rate * access_pool.attack_part_kept_attack * access_target_need
    flow access_new at access_rate * access_pool.attack_part_new:  @attack_pipeline @capability_use
        "The force gains initial access to targets with capabilities that no one has used."
        in_access -> accesses
        in_access_attacks -> access_attacks at access_rate * access_pool.attack_part_new / in_access_size
        access_pool.fresh -> access_pool.held_attack at access_rate * access_pool.attack_part_new * access_target_need
    share in_access limit access_rate  @attack_pipeline
    share in_access_attacks limit access_rate / in_access_size  @attack_pipeline
    flow move_known at movement_rate * movement_pool.one_day:  @attack_pipeline @one_day_chance
        "The force moves inside targets' networks through known flaws."
        accesses -> positioned
        access_attacks -> positioned_attacks at movement_rate * movement_pool.one_day / access_size
    flow move_spy at movement_rate * movement_pool.attack_part_spy:  @attack_pipeline @capability_use
        "The force moves inside targets' networks with capabilities that surveillance uses."
        accesses -> positioned
        access_attacks -> positioned_attacks at movement_rate * movement_pool.attack_part_spy / access_size
        movement_pool.spy_used -> movement_pool.held_shared at movement_rate * movement_pool.attack_part_spy * movement_target_need
    flow move_kept_shared at movement_rate * movement_pool.attack_part_kept_shared:  @attack_pipeline @capability_use
        "The force moves inside targets' networks with kept capabilities of earlier attacks that surveillance
            also uses."
        accesses -> positioned
        access_attacks -> positioned_attacks at movement_rate * movement_pool.attack_part_kept_shared / access_size
        movement_pool.kept_shared -> movement_pool.held_shared at movement_rate * movement_pool.attack_part_kept_shared * movement_target_need
    flow move_kept_attack at movement_rate * movement_pool.attack_part_kept_attack:  @attack_pipeline @capability_use
        "The force moves inside targets' networks with kept capabilities of earlier attacks."
        accesses -> positioned
        access_attacks -> positioned_attacks at movement_rate * movement_pool.attack_part_kept_attack / access_size
        movement_pool.kept_attack -> movement_pool.held_attack at movement_rate * movement_pool.attack_part_kept_attack * movement_target_need
    flow move_new at movement_rate * movement_pool.attack_part_new:  @attack_pipeline @capability_use
        "The force moves inside targets' networks with capabilities that no one has used."
        accesses -> positioned
        access_attacks -> positioned_attacks at movement_rate * movement_pool.attack_part_new / access_size
        movement_pool.fresh -> movement_pool.held_attack at movement_rate * movement_pool.attack_part_new * movement_target_need
    share accesses limit movement_rate  @attack_pipeline
    share access_attacks limit movement_rate / access_size  @attack_pipeline
    # The effect turns the targets of one attack into an attack, with one capability of the effect for each kind
    # of system. An attack built in advance holds its capabilities while it waits; an attack built on demand is
    # made at once, so an exposed one burns them and a hidden one keeps them.
    flow prepare_known at hold_rate * effect_pool.one_day:  @held_capabilities @one_day_chance
        "The force builds an effect in advance through known flaws."
        positioned -> at hold_rate * effect_pool.one_day * positioned_size
        positioned_attacks -> at hold_rate * effect_pool.one_day
        -> held_ready
        -> held_targets at hold_rate * effect_pool.one_day * positioned_size
        -> held_spread at hold_rate * effect_pool.one_day * positioned_size * made_spread
        -> held_outcomes at hold_rate * effect_pool.one_day * positioned_size / made_spread
    flow prepare_kept at hold_rate * effect_pool.attack_part_kept_attack:  @held_capabilities @capability_use
        "The force builds an effect in advance with kept capabilities of earlier attacks."
        positioned -> at hold_rate * effect_pool.attack_part_kept_attack * positioned_size
        positioned_attacks -> at hold_rate * effect_pool.attack_part_kept_attack
        -> held_ready
        -> held_targets at hold_rate * effect_pool.attack_part_kept_attack * positioned_size
        -> held_spread at hold_rate * effect_pool.attack_part_kept_attack * positioned_size * made_spread
        -> held_outcomes at hold_rate * effect_pool.attack_part_kept_attack * positioned_size / made_spread
        effect_pool.kept_attack -> effect_pool.held_attack at hold_rate * effect_pool.attack_part_kept_attack * positioned_types
    flow prepare_new at hold_rate * effect_pool.attack_part_new:  @held_capabilities @capability_use
        "The force builds an effect in advance with capabilities that no one has used."
        positioned -> at hold_rate * effect_pool.attack_part_new * positioned_size
        positioned_attacks -> at hold_rate * effect_pool.attack_part_new
        -> held_ready
        -> held_targets at hold_rate * effect_pool.attack_part_new * positioned_size
        -> held_spread at hold_rate * effect_pool.attack_part_new * positioned_size * made_spread
        -> held_outcomes at hold_rate * effect_pool.attack_part_new * positioned_size / made_spread
        effect_pool.fresh -> effect_pool.held_attack at hold_rate * effect_pool.attack_part_new * positioned_types
    flow now_known at now_rate * effect_pool.one_day:  @attack_pipeline @operator_priority @one_day_chance
        "The force makes an attack built on demand through known flaws as soon as it is ready."
        positioned -> at now_rate * effect_pool.one_day * positioned_size
        positioned_attacks -> at now_rate * effect_pool.one_day
        -> assessing
        -> assessing_targets at now_rate * effect_pool.one_day * positioned_size
        -> assessing_outcomes at now_rate * effect_pool.one_day * positioned_size / made_spread
    flow now_kept at now_rate * effect_pool.attack_part_kept_attack:  @attack_pipeline @capability_burn
        "The force makes an attack built on demand with kept capabilities as soon as it is ready."
        positioned -> at now_rate * effect_pool.attack_part_kept_attack * positioned_size
        positioned_attacks -> at now_rate * effect_pool.attack_part_kept_attack
        -> assessing
        -> assessing_targets at now_rate * effect_pool.attack_part_kept_attack * positioned_size
        -> assessing_outcomes at now_rate * effect_pool.attack_part_kept_attack * positioned_size / made_spread
        effect_pool.kept_attack -> effect_pool.burned at now_rate * effect_pool.attack_part_kept_attack * positioned_types * exposure
    flow now_new at now_rate * effect_pool.attack_part_new:  @attack_pipeline @capability_burn
        "The force makes an attack built on demand with capabilities that no one has used as soon as it is
            ready."
        positioned -> at now_rate * effect_pool.attack_part_new * positioned_size
        positioned_attacks -> at now_rate * effect_pool.attack_part_new
        -> assessing
        -> assessing_targets at now_rate * effect_pool.attack_part_new * positioned_size
        -> assessing_outcomes at now_rate * effect_pool.attack_part_new * positioned_size / made_spread
        effect_pool.fresh -> effect_pool.burned at now_rate * effect_pool.attack_part_new * positioned_types * exposure
        effect_pool.fresh -> effect_pool.kept_attack at now_rate * effect_pool.attack_part_new * positioned_types * (1
            - exposure)
    share positioned limit (hold_rate + now_rate) * positioned_size  @attack_pipeline
    share positioned_attacks limit hold_rate + now_rate  @attack_pipeline
    flow use_held at moment_rate * held_ready * execution_served:  @held_capabilities @operator_priority
        "The moment of an attack held ready comes, and the force makes it."
        held_ready -> assessing
        held_targets -> assessing_targets at moment_rate * held_targets * execution_served
        held_spread -> at moment_rate * held_spread * execution_served
        held_outcomes -> assessing_outcomes at moment_rate * held_outcomes * execution_served
    flow assessment_ends at assessed_rate:  @assessment_time
        "The damage assessment of an attack ends, and its lessons count."
        assessing ->
        assessing_targets -> at assessed_rate * assessed_size
        assessing_outcomes -> at assessed_rate * assessing_outcomes / max(assessing, 0.001 operations)
    let access_learn_rate: accesses/year = (
        access_rate * access_pool.attack_served * (1 accesses) / (1 targets) + task_overlap
        * surveillance_program.access_rate * access_pool.spy_served
    )  @access_lessons
        "Lessons of access that the force gains in a year: the accesses built for attacks, and the accesses
            that surveillance gains as far as the tasks overlap"
    let access_lessons_now: accesses = (
        (attacks_now * targets_now * (1 accesses) / (1 targets) + task_overlap
        * surveillance_program.builds_now) / access_forget_rate
    )  @access_lessons
        "Lessons of access that the force keeps today"
    let access_factor: ratio = exp(clip(ln(1 - access_learning) / ln_two
        * ln(access_know_how / access_lessons_now), -700, 700))  @access_lessons
        "Work of access as a multiple of today's, from the lessons of access"
    var access_know_how: accesses = access_lessons_now in 1 accesses to 10000000000 accesses size 10000 accesses  @access_lessons
        "Lessons of access that the force keeps: accesses built"
    flow learn_access: -> access_know_how at access_learn_rate  @access_lessons
        "The force learns from the accesses that it and surveillance build."
    flow forget_access: access_know_how -> at access_know_how * access_forget_rate  @access_lessons
        "The force loses lessons of access to patches, turnover and the disclosure of its tools."
    flow learn: -> know_how at learn_rate  @learning_basis
        "The force learns from the assessed outcomes of its attacks."
    flow forget: know_how -> at loss_rate  @forgetting
        "The force loses lessons to patches, burned tools, staff turnover and secrecy."
    flow tally: -> attacks_made at attacks  @attack_count
        "The force makes attacks."
    event start when t >= 0 years:  @period_start
        "The period starts in 2026, with today's split of the country's cyber experts."

Stage:  @stage_basis
    "A kind of work that an attack needs. AI does a growing share of it."
    role effects_program
    input work: person-years/operation = 1 person-years/operation in 0.05 person-years/operation to 10 person-years/operation  @stage_work
        "Expert work by hand for one attack, apart from its targets"
    input target_work: person-years/target = 0.5 person-years/target in 0 person-years/target to 10 person-years/target  @stage_work
        "Expert work by hand for each target of an attack"
    input ai_now: fraction = 0 in 0 to 0.9  @stage_ai
        "Share of the work that AI does today"
    input ai_end: fraction = 0.5 in 0 to 0.95  @stage_ai
        "Share of the work that AI does at the end of AI progress"
    input payload_path: bool = false  @stage_paths
        "The work moves with AI progress on payloads, which is slower than progress on access"
    input split_ai: bool = false  @stage_split
        "The work for each target has its own AI share, apart from the work for the attack"
    input target_ai_now: fraction = 0 in 0 to 0.9  @stage_split
        "Share of the work for each target that AI does today"
    input target_ai_end: fraction = 0.5 in 0 to 0.9  @stage_split
        "Share of the work for each target that AI does at the end of AI progress"
    input target_payload_path: bool = false  @stage_split
        "The work for each target moves with AI progress on payloads, which is slower than progress on access"
    input held: bool = false  @waiting_cost
        "The work's product waits ready for its use, decays and needs upkeep"
    input builds: bool = false  @lead_time
        "The work's time counts in the time to build an attack"
    input time_now: years = 0.1 years in 0.003 years to 5 years  @lead_time
        "Time that the work takes today"
    input time_end: years = 0.1 years in 0.001 years to 5 years  @lead_time
        "Time that the work takes at the end of AI progress"
    let progress: fraction = (ai_tools.payload_progress if payload_path else ai_tools.progress)  @stage_paths
        "AI progress that reaches the work"
    let ai_share: fraction = ai_now + (ai_end - ai_now) * progress  @stage_ai
        "Share of the work that AI does"
    let stage_targets: targets/operation = effects_program.attack_targets  @stage_work
        "Targets of the attacks that the work serves"
    let hand_work: person-years/operation = work + target_work * stage_targets  @stage_work
        "Expert work by hand for one attack with its targets"
    let hand_work_now: person-years/operation = work + target_work * effects_program.targets_now  @stage_work
        "The same work for one attack today"
    let overhead: ratio = (
        effects_program.supervision / effects_program.parallel + effects_program.compute
        / leadership.expert_cost
    )  @stage_ai
        "Cost of a unit of AI work as a share of its cost by hand: the supervision and the compute"
    let overhead_now: ratio = (
        effects_program.supervision / effects_program.parallel_now + effects_program.compute_now
        / leadership.expert_cost
    )  @stage_ai
        "The same share today"
    let unit_cost: ratio = 1 - ai_share + ai_share * overhead  @stage_ai
        "Cost of the work for the attack as a share of its cost by hand: the human part and the AI part"
    let unit_cost_now: ratio = 1 - ai_now + ai_now * overhead_now  @stage_ai
        "The same share today"
    let target_progress: fraction = (
        (ai_tools.payload_progress if target_payload_path else ai_tools.progress) if split_ai else progress
    )  @stage_split
        "AI progress that reaches the work for each target"
    let target_share: fraction = (
        (target_ai_now + (target_ai_end - target_ai_now) * target_progress) if split_ai else ai_share
    )  @stage_split
        "Share of the work for each target that AI does"
    let target_share_now: fraction = (target_ai_now if split_ai else ai_now)  @stage_split
        "The same share today"
    let target_cost: ratio = 1 - target_share + target_share * overhead  @stage_split
        "Cost of the work for each target as a share of its cost by hand"
    let target_cost_now: ratio = 1 - target_share_now + target_share_now * overhead_now  @stage_split
        "The same share today"
    let cost: person-years/operation = (
        work * unit_cost + target_work * stage_targets * target_cost
    ) * (effects_program.waiting if held else 1)  @stage_cost
        "Expert work of the stage for one attack"
    let cost_now: person-years/operation = (
        work * unit_cost_now + target_work * effects_program.targets_now * target_cost_now
    ) * (effects_program.waiting_now if held else 1)  @stage_cost
        "The same work today"
    input shared_work: bool = false  @access_lessons
        "The work is the same as surveillance's: its lessons come from attacks and from surveillance"
    let learning: ratio = (
        effects_program.access_factor if shared_work else 1 - effects_program.cost_learning
        * effects_program.gain
    )  @cost_learning
        "Share of the stage's work that is left after lessons"
    let learned_cost: person-years/operation = cost * learning  @cost_learning
        "Expert work of the stage for one attack, after lessons"
    input analyst: bool = false  @expert_pools
        "Analysts do the work, not operators"
    let operator_cost: person-years/operation = (
        0 person-years/operation if analyst else learned_cost
    )  @expert_pools
        "Operators' work of the stage for one attack, after lessons"
    let operator_cost_now: person-years/operation = (
        0 person-years/operation if analyst else cost_now
    )  @expert_pools
        "The same work today"
    let analyst_cost_now: person-years/operation = (
        cost_now if analyst else 0 person-years/operation
    )  @expert_pools
        "Analysts' work of the stage for one attack today"
    let effects_cost: person-years/operation = (
        0 person-years/operation if shared_work or analyst else cost
    )  @cost_learning
        "The operators' work of the stage before lessons, where the lessons of attacks cut it"
    let effects_cost_now: person-years/operation = (
        0 person-years/operation if shared_work or analyst else cost_now
    )  @cost_learning
        "The same work today"
    let ai_work: person-years/operation = (
        work * ai_share + target_work * stage_targets * target_share
    )  @stage_ai
        "Work of one attack that AI does, as the expert work by hand that it replaces"
    let time: years = time_now * exp(progress * ln(time_end / time_now))  @lead_time
        "Time that the work takes"
    let lead_term: years = (time if builds else 0 years)  @lead_time
        "The work's part of the time to build an attack"
    let lead_term_now: years = (time_now if builds else 0 years)  @lead_time
        "The same part today"

planning_stage: Stage
    "Target analysis, legal review and approval."
    analyst = true  @expert_pools
    work = 1 person-years/operation  @planning_stage
    target_work = 0.5 person-years/target  @planning_stage
    ai_end = 0.2  @planning_stage
    payload_path = true  @planning_stage
    split_ai = true  @planning_ai
    target_ai_now = 0.2  @planning_ai
    target_ai_end = 0.75  @planning_ai

access_stage: Stage
    "Gaining and keeping access to the targets."
    work = 0.3 person-years/operation  @access_stage
    target_work = 1.2 person-years/target  @access_stage
    ai_now = 0.25  @access_stage
    ai_end = 0.87  @access_stage
    held = true  @access_stage
    builds = true  @access_stage
    time_now = 0.55 years  @access_stage
    time_end = 0.01 years  @access_stage
    shared_work = true  @access_lessons

payload_stage: Stage
    "Building and testing the effect."
    work = 1.6 person-years/operation  @payload_stage
    target_work = 0.4 person-years/target  @payload_stage
    ai_now = 0.05  @payload_stage
    ai_end = 0.57  @payload_stage
    payload_path = true  @payload_stage
    held = true  @payload_stage
    builds = true  @payload_stage
    time_now = 0.2 years  @payload_stage
    time_end = 0.07 years  @payload_stage

execution_stage: Stage
    "Running the attack at its time."
    work = 0.4 person-years/operation  @execution_stage
    target_work = 0.1 person-years/target  @execution_stage
    ai_now = 0.3  @execution_stage
    ai_end = 0.9  @execution_stage

assessment_stage: Stage
    "Damage assessment."
    analyst = true  @expert_pools
    work = 1 person-years/operation  @assessment_stage
    target_work = 0.7 person-years/target  @assessment_stage
    ai_end = 0.3  @assessment_stage
    payload_path = true  @assessment_stage
    split_ai = true  @assessment_ai
    target_ai_now = 0.1  @assessment_ai
    target_ai_end = 0.6  @assessment_ai
    target_payload_path = true  @assessment_ai
    time_now = 0.33 years  @assessment_time
    time_end = 0.16 years  @assessment_time
    let stage_targets: targets/operation = effects_program.assessed_size  @assessment_time

Pool:  @capability_pool
    "The capabilities of one step of an operation. A capability is a flaw that the defenders do not know, with
        the tools that use it, and it burns as a whole. It is never used, used by surveillance, used in an
        attack, or burned. Used in an attack, it is held by an attack underway, or kept from an earlier attack
        that was not exposed."
    role researchers
    input known: fraction = 0.8 in 0 to 0.99  @one_day_chance
        "Share of the needs of the step that a known flaw or the target's own functions can serve"
    input patch_pace: ratio = 1 in 0 to 2  @one_day_chance
        "Pace at which targets fix known flaws at this step, as a multiple of the defenders' fix rate"
    let step_time: years = access_stage.time  @one_day_chance
        "Time from the start of the step until the force uses the flaw"
    let step_time_now: years = access_stage.time_now  @one_day_chance
        "The same time today"
    let open: fraction = (
        defenders.residual + (1 - defenders.residual) * exp(-defenders.fix_rate * patch_pace * step_time)
    )  @one_day_chance
        "Share of targets that have not fixed a known flaw by the time of its use"
    let open_now: fraction = (
        defenders.residual + (1 - defenders.residual) * exp(-defenders.fix_rate * patch_pace * step_time_now)
    )  @one_day_chance
        "The same share today"
    let one_day: fraction = known * open  @one_day_chance
        "Chance that a known flaw serves a need of the step: a one-day, which takes nothing from the pool"
    let one_day_now: fraction = known * open_now  @one_day_chance
        "The same chance today"
    # What the missions need from the pool: the zero-days of the units that pass the step. Each instance says.
    let attack_need: capabilities/year = 0 capabilities/year  @capability_use
        "Zero-days that attacks need in a year at the step, for attacks that wait before they are made"
    let attack_need_now: capabilities/year = 0 capabilities/year  @capability_use
        "Zero-days that attacks that wait need in a year at the step today"
    let at_once_need: capabilities/year = 0 capabilities/year  @capability_use
        "Zero-days that attacks need in a year at the step, for attacks made as soon as they are ready"
    let at_once_need_now: capabilities/year = 0 capabilities/year  @capability_use
        "Zero-days that attacks made at once need in a year at the step today"
    let spy_need: capabilities/year = 0 capabilities/year  @capability_use
        "Zero-days that surveillance needs in a year at the step"
    let spy_need_now: capabilities/year = 0 capabilities/year  @capability_use
        "Zero-days that surveillance needs in a year at the step today"
    let holders: operations = 0 operations  @capability_burn
        "Attacks underway that hold capabilities of the pool"
    let holders_now: operations = 0 operations  @capability_burn
        "Attacks underway today that hold capabilities of the pool"
    let made: operations/year = 0 operations/year  @capability_burn
        "Attacks made in a year that hold capabilities of the pool"
    let made_now: operations/year = 0 operations/year  @capability_burn
        "Attacks made in a year today that hold capabilities of the pool"
    let made_rate: per_year = made / max(holders, 0.001 operations)  @capability_burn
        "Share of the attacks that hold capabilities of the pool that the force makes in a year"
    let made_rate_now: per_year = made_now / max(holders_now, 0.001 operations)  @capability_burn
        "The same share today"
    # Today's shares of the sources of a zero-day. A source that the step does not have is zero.
    let attack_spy_now: fraction = effects_program.spy_source  @capability_sources
        "Share of an attack's zero-days at the step today that surveillance already uses"
    let attack_kept_now: fraction = effects_program.reuse_source  @capability_sources
        "Share of an attack's zero-days at the step today that an earlier attack used and did not burn"
    let spy_own_now: fraction = surveillance_program.own_source  @capability_sources
        "Share of surveillance's zero-days at the step today that it already uses"
    let spy_kept_now: fraction = surveillance_program.attack_source  @capability_sources
        "Share of surveillance's zero-days at the step today that an earlier attack used and did not burn"
    # Today is a rest point of the pool: each stock's inflows equal its outflows at today's needs and shares.
    let held_now: capabilities = attack_need_now / (made_rate_now
        + defenders.use_rediscovery_now)  @capability_rest
        "Capabilities held by attacks underway today"
    # The kept capabilities that surveillance also uses are in use, so they are lost at the in-use rate, and
    # the others at the stockpile rate. Their balances give the shared share as the positive root of
    # B·Δ·φ² + b·φ − Q·δ_s = 0, with b = δ_u·R + B·δ_s − Q·Δ and Δ = δ_u − δ_s.
    let kept_net_now: capabilities/year = (
        (1 - effects_program.exposure) * (made_rate_now * held_now + at_once_need_now
        * (1 - attack_spy_now - attack_kept_now)) - (attack_need_now + effects_program.exposure
        * at_once_need_now) * attack_kept_now
    )  @capability_rest
        "Capabilities that the kept stock gains today, less the attacks' draws from it"
    let kept_shared_in_now: capabilities/year = (
        (1 - effects_program.exposure) * made_rate_now * attack_need_now * attack_spy_now
        / (made_rate_now + defenders.use_rediscovery_now) + spy_need_now * spy_kept_now
    )  @capability_rest
        "Capabilities that enter the kept stock that surveillance also uses, today"
    let kept_shared_draw_now: capabilities/year = (
        attack_need_now * attack_kept_now * (1 - (1 - effects_program.exposure) * made_rate_now
        / (made_rate_now + defenders.use_rediscovery_now))
    )  @capability_rest
        "Net draw of attacks on the kept stock that surveillance also uses, for each unit of its share"
    let use_gap_now: per_year = defenders.use_rediscovery_now - defenders.rediscovery_now  @capability_rest
        "How much faster a flaw in use is lost than a stockpiled flaw, today"
    let kept_root_b: per_year = (
        defenders.use_rediscovery_now * kept_net_now + kept_shared_draw_now * defenders.rediscovery_now
        - kept_shared_in_now * use_gap_now
    ) / (1 capabilities/year)  @capability_rest
        "Linear term of the balance of the kept stock that surveillance also uses, per capability a year"
    let kept_shared_part_now: fraction = (
        2 * kept_shared_in_now / (1 capabilities/year) * defenders.rediscovery_now
        / max(kept_root_b + sqrt(kept_root_b ** 2 + 4 * kept_shared_draw_now / (1 capabilities/year)
        * use_gap_now * kept_shared_in_now / (1 capabilities/year) * defenders.rediscovery_now),
        1e-30 per_year)
    )  @capability_rest
        "Share of the kept capabilities today that surveillance also uses"
    let kept_now: capabilities = kept_net_now / (defenders.rediscovery_now + kept_shared_part_now
        * use_gap_now)  @capability_rest
        "Capabilities kept today from earlier attacks that were not exposed"
    let kept_shared_now: capabilities = kept_shared_part_now * kept_now  @capability_rest
        "Kept capabilities today that surveillance also uses"
    let kept_attack_now: capabilities = kept_now - kept_shared_now  @capability_rest
        "Kept capabilities today that only attacks use"
    let held_shared_now: capabilities = (
        attack_need_now * (attack_spy_now + attack_kept_now * kept_shared_part_now)
        / (made_rate_now + defenders.use_rediscovery_now)
    )  @capability_rest
        "Capabilities held by attacks underway today that surveillance also uses"
    let held_attack_now: capabilities = held_now - held_shared_now  @capability_rest
        "Capabilities held by attacks underway today that only attacks use"
    let spy_used_now: capabilities = (
        spy_need_now * (1 - spy_own_now - spy_kept_now) - attack_need_now * attack_spy_now
    ) / defenders.use_rediscovery_now  @capability_rest
        "Capabilities that surveillance uses today and that no attack has used"
    let fresh_now: capabilities = researchers.reserve_time * ((attack_need_now + at_once_need_now)
        * (1 - attack_spy_now - attack_kept_now) + spy_need_now
        * (1 - spy_own_now - spy_kept_now))  @capability_reserve
        "Capabilities never used today"
    assert attack_spy_now <= 0 or spy_used_now > 0 capabilities  @capability_rest
        "Attacks take more of surveillance's capabilities today than surveillance draws: lower the share of an
            attack's zero-days that surveillance already uses."
    assert attack_kept_now <= 0 or kept_now > 0 capabilities  @capability_rest
        "Attacks reuse more capabilities today than the attacks that stay hidden keep: lower the share of an
            attack's zero-days that it reuses."
    assert spy_kept_now <= 0 or kept_attack_now > 0 capabilities  @capability_rest
        "Surveillance takes more of the attacks' kept capabilities today than they keep: lower the share of
            surveillance's zero-days that come from attacks."
    # A need is served by a capability already in use as if each capability in use were one independent try:
    # with chance 1 - exp(-y), where y grows in proportion to the capabilities in use and today's y gives
    # today's shares. The rest of the need takes a never-used capability.
    let attack_scale_now: ratio = -ln(1 - attack_spy_now - attack_kept_now)  @capability_sources
        "Tries of an attack's need on the capabilities already in use today"
    let kept: capabilities = kept_shared + kept_attack  @capability_pool
        "Capabilities kept from earlier attacks that were not exposed"
    let kept_shared_share: fraction = kept_shared / max(kept, 1e-9 capabilities)  @capability_pool
        "Share of the kept capabilities that surveillance also uses"
    let attack_spy_tries: ratio = (
        attack_scale_now * attack_spy_now / max(attack_spy_now + attack_kept_now, 1e-12) * spy_used
        / max(spy_used_now, 1e-6 capabilities)
    )  @capability_sources
        "Tries of an attack's need on surveillance's capabilities"
    let attack_kept_tries: ratio = (
        attack_scale_now * attack_kept_now / max(attack_spy_now + attack_kept_now, 1e-12) * kept
        / max(kept_now, 1e-6 capabilities)
    )  @capability_sources
        "Tries of an attack's need on the kept capabilities"
    let attack_tries: ratio = attack_spy_tries + attack_kept_tries  @capability_sources
        "Tries of an attack's need on the capabilities already in use"
    let attack_from_spy: fraction = -expm1(-attack_tries) * attack_spy_tries / max(attack_tries,
        1e-12)  @capability_sources
        "Share of an attack's zero-days that surveillance already uses"
    let attack_from_kept: fraction = -expm1(-attack_tries) * attack_kept_tries / max(attack_tries,
        1e-12)  @capability_sources
        "Share of an attack's zero-days that an earlier attack used and did not burn"
    let attack_from_fresh: fraction = exp(-attack_tries)  @capability_sources
        "Share of an attack's zero-days that are never used"
    let spy_scale_now: ratio = -ln(1 - spy_own_now - spy_kept_now)  @capability_sources
        "Tries of surveillance's need on the capabilities already in use today"
    let spy_in_use: capabilities = spy_used + held_shared + kept_shared  @capability_pool
        "Capabilities that surveillance uses"
    let spy_in_use_now: capabilities = spy_used_now + held_shared_now + kept_shared_now  @capability_rest
        "The same capabilities today"
    let spy_own_tries: ratio = (
        spy_scale_now * spy_own_now / max(spy_own_now + spy_kept_now, 1e-12) * spy_in_use
        / max(spy_in_use_now, 1e-6 capabilities)
    )  @capability_sources
        "Tries of surveillance's need on its own capabilities"
    let spy_kept_tries: ratio = (
        spy_scale_now * spy_kept_now / max(spy_own_now + spy_kept_now, 1e-12) * kept_attack
        / max(kept_attack_now, 1e-6 capabilities)
    )  @capability_sources
        "Tries of surveillance's need on the capabilities that only attacks used"
    let spy_tries: ratio = spy_own_tries + spy_kept_tries  @capability_sources
        "Tries of surveillance's need on the capabilities already in use"
    let spy_from_kept: fraction = -expm1(-spy_tries) * spy_kept_tries / max(spy_tries,
        1e-12)  @capability_sources
        "Share of surveillance's zero-days that an earlier attack used and did not burn"
    let spy_from_fresh: fraction = exp(-spy_tries)  @capability_sources
        "Share of surveillance's zero-days that are never used"
    # The parts of an attack step's units by the source of their zero-days. Each part moves with its source.
    let attack_part_spy: fraction = (1 - one_day) * attack_from_spy  @capability_sources
        "Share of the units of an attack step whose zero-days surveillance already uses"
    let attack_part_kept_shared: fraction = (
        1 - one_day
    ) * attack_from_kept * kept_shared_share  @capability_sources
        "Share of the units of an attack step that reuse kept capabilities that surveillance also uses"
    let attack_part_kept_attack: fraction = (
        1 - one_day
    ) * attack_from_kept * (1 - kept_shared_share)  @capability_sources
        "Share of the units of an attack step that reuse kept capabilities that only attacks used"
    let fresh_draws: capabilities/year = (
        (attack_need + at_once_need) * attack_from_fresh + spy_need * spy_from_fresh
    )  @capability_pool
        "Never-used capabilities that the missions ask from the pool in a year"
    let available: fraction = min(1, fresh / max(fresh_draws * researchers.supply_time,
        1e-9 capabilities))  @capability_pool
        "Share of the asks for never-used capabilities that the pool serves now"
    let attack_served: fraction = 1 - (1 - one_day) * attack_from_fresh * (1 - available)  @capability_pool
        "Share of the units of an attack step that the pool lets pass now"
    let spy_served: fraction = 1 - (1 - one_day) * spy_from_fresh * (1 - available)  @capability_pool
        "Share of the units of a surveillance step that the pool lets pass now"
    let attack_part_new: fraction = (1 - one_day) * attack_from_fresh * available  @capability_sources
        "Share of the units of an attack step whose zero-days no one has used"
    # The parts of a surveillance step's units by the source of their zero-days. Each part moves with its source.
    let spy_part_free: fraction = one_day + (1 - one_day) * (1 - spy_from_kept
        - spy_from_fresh)  @capability_sources
        "Share of the units of a surveillance step that take nothing from the pool: one-days, and capabilities
            that surveillance already uses"
    let spy_part_kept: fraction = (1 - one_day) * spy_from_kept  @capability_sources
        "Share of the units of a surveillance step that take kept capabilities of earlier attacks"
    let spy_part_new: fraction = (1 - one_day) * spy_from_fresh * available  @capability_sources
        "Share of the units of a surveillance step whose zero-days no one has used"
    let in_attacks: capabilities = held_shared + held_attack + kept  @capability_pool
        "Capabilities used in an attack and not burned"
    # Exposed attacks burn the capabilities that they hold. Where surveillance also uses one, the defenders find
    # a share of the accesses that run on it.
    let burn_loss: per_year = (
        surveillance_program.find_share * effects_program.exposure * made_rate * held_shared * (1 - one_day)
        / max(spy_in_use, 0.001 capabilities)
    )  @capability_burn
        "Share of surveillance's accesses that the defenders find in a year through the capabilities that
            exposed attacks burn and surveillance also uses"
    let burn_loss_now: per_year = (
        surveillance_program.find_share * effects_program.exposure * made_rate_now * held_shared_now
        * (1 - one_day_now) / max(spy_in_use_now, 0.001 capabilities)
    )  @capability_burn
        "The same share today"
    let research_need: capabilities/year = max(fresh_draws + defenders.rediscovery * fresh
        + (researchers.reserve_time * fresh_draws - fresh) / researchers.reserve_time,
        0 capabilities/year)  @research_split
        "Capabilities that the pool asks from the researchers in a year: those that the missions take from it,
            and those that others find"
    let research_need_now: capabilities/year = (
        (attack_need_now + at_once_need_now) * (1 - attack_spy_now - attack_kept_now) + spy_need_now
        * (1 - spy_own_now - spy_kept_now) + defenders.rediscovery_now * fresh_now
    )  @research_split
        "The same capabilities today"
    var fresh: capabilities = fresh_now in 0 capabilities to 10000000 capabilities size 100 capabilities  @capability_pool
        "Capabilities never used"
    var spy_used: capabilities = spy_used_now in 0 capabilities to 10000000 capabilities size 100 capabilities  @capability_pool
        "Capabilities that surveillance uses and that no attack has used"
    var held_shared: capabilities = held_shared_now in 0 capabilities to 10000000 capabilities size 100 capabilities  @capability_pool
        "Capabilities held by attacks underway that surveillance also uses"
    var held_attack: capabilities = held_attack_now in 0 capabilities to 10000000 capabilities size 100 capabilities  @capability_pool
        "Capabilities held by attacks underway that only attacks use"
    var kept_shared: capabilities = kept_shared_now in 0 capabilities to 10000000 capabilities size 100 capabilities  @capability_pool
        "Capabilities kept from earlier attacks that were not exposed, which surveillance also uses"
    var kept_attack: capabilities = kept_attack_now in 0 capabilities to 10000000 capabilities size 100 capabilities  @capability_pool
        "Capabilities kept from earlier attacks that were not exposed, which only attacks use"
    var burned: capabilities = 0 capabilities size 10000 capabilities  @capability_burn
        "Capabilities that exposed attacks burned since the start"
    flow research: -> fresh at research_need * min(1, researchers.output
        / max(researchers.need, 1e-9 capabilities/year))  @research_split
        "The researchers find new capabilities for the pool."
    flow burn_shared: held_shared -> burned at effects_program.exposure * made_rate * held_shared  @capability_burn
        "Exposed attacks burn the capabilities that they hold and that surveillance also uses."
    flow keep_shared: held_shared -> kept_shared at (
        1 - effects_program.exposure
    ) * made_rate * held_shared  @capability_burn
        "Attacks that stay hidden keep them."
    flow burn_attack: held_attack -> burned at effects_program.exposure * made_rate * held_attack  @capability_burn
        "Exposed attacks burn the capabilities that they hold and that only attacks use."
    flow keep_attack: held_attack -> kept_attack at (
        1 - effects_program.exposure
    ) * made_rate * held_attack  @capability_burn
        "Attacks that stay hidden keep them."
    flow found_fresh: fresh -> at defenders.rediscovery * fresh  @rediscovery
        "Others find and disclose flaws that no one has used."
    flow found_spy: spy_used -> at defenders.use_rediscovery * spy_used  @rediscovery
        "Others find and disclose flaws that surveillance uses."
    flow found_held_shared: held_shared -> at defenders.use_rediscovery * held_shared  @rediscovery
        "Others find and disclose flaws that attacks underway hold."
    flow found_held_attack: held_attack -> at defenders.use_rediscovery * held_attack  @rediscovery
        "Others find and disclose flaws that attacks underway hold."
    flow found_kept_shared: kept_shared -> at defenders.use_rediscovery * kept_shared  @rediscovery
        "Others find and disclose flaws that attacks kept."
    flow found_kept_attack: kept_attack -> at defenders.rediscovery * kept_attack  @rediscovery
        "Others find and disclose flaws that attacks kept."
    # Each limit is all that the flows ask, so it caps none of them; at an empty stock they share what is left.
    share fresh limit fresh_draws * available + defenders.rediscovery * fresh  @capability_pool
    share spy_used limit attack_need * attack_from_spy + defenders.use_rediscovery * spy_used  @capability_pool
    share held_shared limit (made_rate + defenders.use_rediscovery) * held_shared  @capability_pool
    share held_attack limit (made_rate + defenders.use_rediscovery) * held_attack  @capability_pool
    share kept_shared limit (attack_need * attack_from_kept * kept_shared_share + defenders.use_rediscovery
        * kept_shared)  @capability_pool
    share kept_attack limit (attack_need * attack_from_kept * (1 - kept_shared_share) + at_once_need
        * attack_from_kept * effects_program.exposure + spy_need * spy_from_kept + defenders.rediscovery
        * kept_attack)  @capability_pool

access_pool: Pool
    "Capabilities that get access to a target network. Surveillance and attacks share them."
    let step_time: years = effects_program.initial_time  @one_day_chance
    let step_time_now: years = effects_program.initial_time_now  @one_day_chance
    let attack_need: capabilities/year = effects_program.access_rate * effects_program.access_target_need * (1
        - one_day)  @capability_use
    let attack_need_now: capabilities/year = effects_program.attacks_now * effects_program.types_now * (1
        - one_day_now)  @capability_use
    let spy_need: capabilities/year = surveillance_program.access_rate * (1 capabilities/access) * (1
        - one_day)  @capability_use
    let spy_need_now: capabilities/year = surveillance_program.builds_now * (1 capabilities/access) * (1
        - one_day_now)  @capability_use
    let holders: operations = (
        effects_program.access_attacks + effects_program.positioned_attacks + effects_program.held_ready
    )  @capability_burn
    let holders_now: operations = effects_program.attacks_now * (effects_program.movement_time_now
        + payload_stage.time_now + effects_program.built_ahead_now
        / effects_program.use_rate)  @capability_burn
    let made: operations/year = effects_program.attacks  @capability_burn
    let made_now: operations/year = effects_program.attacks_now  @capability_burn

movement_pool: Pool
    "Capabilities that move inside a network from the first access to the systems that the operation needs.
        Surveillance and attacks share them."
    known = 0.9  @one_day_chance
    patch_pace = 0.5  @one_day_chance
    let step_time: years = effects_program.movement_time  @one_day_chance
    let step_time_now: years = effects_program.movement_time_now  @one_day_chance
    let attack_need: capabilities/year = effects_program.movement_rate * effects_program.movement_target_need * (1
        - one_day)  @capability_use
    let attack_need_now: capabilities/year = effects_program.attacks_now * effects_program.types_now * (1
        - one_day_now)  @capability_use
    let spy_need: capabilities/year = surveillance_program.deploy_rate * (1 capabilities/access) * (1
        - one_day)  @capability_use
    let spy_need_now: capabilities/year = surveillance_program.deployed_now * (1 capabilities/access) * (1
        - one_day_now)  @capability_use
    let holders: operations = (
        effects_program.positioned_attacks + effects_program.held_ready
    )  @capability_burn
    let holders_now: operations = effects_program.attacks_now * (payload_stage.time_now
        + effects_program.built_ahead_now / effects_program.use_rate)  @capability_burn
    let made: operations/year = effects_program.attacks  @capability_burn
    let made_now: operations/year = effects_program.attacks_now  @capability_burn

effect_pool: Pool
    "Capabilities that give the effect of an attack on the systems of its targets. Only attacks use them."
    patch_pace = 0.2  @one_day_chance
    let step_time: years = payload_stage.time  @one_day_chance
    let step_time_now: years = payload_stage.time_now  @one_day_chance
    let attack_need: capabilities/year = effects_program.hold_rate * effects_program.positioned_types * (1
        - one_day)  @capability_use
    let attack_need_now: capabilities/year = (
        effects_program.built_ahead_now * effects_program.attacks_now * effects_program.types_now
        * (1 - one_day_now)
    )  @capability_use
    let at_once_need: capabilities/year = effects_program.now_rate * effects_program.positioned_types * (1
        - one_day)  @capability_use
    let at_once_need_now: capabilities/year = (
        (1 - effects_program.built_ahead_now) * effects_program.attacks_now * effects_program.types_now
        * (1 - one_day_now)
    )  @capability_use
    let holders: operations = effects_program.held_ready  @capability_burn
    let holders_now: operations = effects_program.built_ahead_now * effects_program.attacks_now / effects_program.use_rate  @capability_burn
    let made: operations/year = effects_program.moment_rate * effects_program.held_ready * effects_program.execution_served  @capability_burn
    let made_now: operations/year = effects_program.built_ahead_now * effects_program.attacks_now  @capability_burn
    let attack_spy_now: fraction = 0  @capability_sources
    let spy_own_now: fraction = 0  @capability_sources
    let spy_kept_now: fraction = 0  @capability_sources

collection_pool: Pool
    "Capabilities that collect what surveillance needs from a network and send it out. Only surveillance uses
        them."
    known = 0.9  @one_day_chance
    patch_pace = 0  @one_day_chance
    let spy_need: capabilities/year = surveillance_program.deploy_rate * (1 capabilities/access) * (1
        - one_day)  @capability_use
    let spy_need_now: capabilities/year = surveillance_program.deployed_now * (1 capabilities/access) * (1
        - one_day_now)  @capability_use
    let attack_spy_now: fraction = 0  @capability_sources
    let attack_kept_now: fraction = 0  @capability_sources
    let spy_kept_now: fraction = 0  @capability_sources

Channel:  @attribution_channels
    "A kind of evidence by which other countries attribute an attack."
    role other_states
    input chance_now: fraction = 0.2 in 0 to 0.9  @attribution_channels
        "Chance today that this evidence attributes an attack"
    input chance_end: fraction = 0.2 in 0 to 0.9  @attribution_channels
        "The same chance at the end of AI progress"
    let chance: fraction = chance_now + (chance_end - chance_now) * ai_tools.progress  @attribution_channels
        "Chance that this evidence attributes an attack"
    let miss: ratio = ln(1 - chance)  @attribution_channels
        "Log-chance that this evidence does not attribute an attack"
    let miss_now: ratio = ln(1 - chance_now)  @attribution_channels
        "The same log-chance today"

tools_channel: Channel
    "The code and tools of an attack."
    chance_now = 0.4  @tools_channel
    chance_end = 0.05  @tools_channel

infrastructure_channel: Channel
    "The servers and accounts that an attack uses."
    chance_now = 0.3  @infrastructure_channel
    chance_end = 0.3  @infrastructure_channel

operators_channel: Channel
    "The habits of the operators: their hours, their language, their mistakes."

targeting_channel: Channel
    "The choice of targets and of the time, which points to whom the attack serves."

output attacks_per_year: operations/year = effects_program.attacks  @attack_pipeline
    "Attacks that the force makes in a year"
    level
output attacks_started: operations/year = effects_program.starts  @attack_count
    "Attacks that the force starts to build in a year"
    level
output capabilities_held: operations = effects_program.held_ready  @held_capabilities
    "Attacks held ready for their moment"
    level
output targets_in_build: targets = (
    effects_program.in_access + effects_program.accesses + effects_program.positioned
)  @attack_pipeline
    "Targets that the force works on before their attacks are ready: gaining access, moving inside the network
        or building the effect"
    level
output attacks_in_assessment: operations = effects_program.assessing  @assessment_time
    "Attacks whose damage the force is assessing"
    level
output share_on_attacks: fraction = leadership.attack_share  @split_balance
    "Share of the operators on attacks"
    level
output expert_ratio: ratio = surveillance_program.spying_experts / leadership.attack_experts  @split_balance
    "Operators on surveillance for each operator on attacks"
    level
output value_to_cost: ratio = (
    effects_program.attack_value / (effects_program.attack_cost + leadership.attack_experts
    * surveillance_program.spying_return)
)  @value_basis
    "Value of the attacks against their full cost: sanctions, burned access and the surveillance that the
        operators on attacks do not do"
    level
output lesson_weight: fraction = effects_program.weight  @learning_curve
    "Weight of lessons in the force's choices"
    level
output plan_chance: fraction = military_planners.best_chance  @plan_test
    "Chance that the best plan of cyber attacks meets its plan"
    level
output best_plan_length: days = military_planners.best_length  @plan_test
    "Length of the best plan of cyber attacks"
    level
output passes: ratio = military_planners.passes_in_a_row  @doctrine_time
    "Planning reviews in a row that a plan passes"
    level
output misses: ratio = military_planners.misses_in_a_row  @doctrine_lapse
    "Planning reviews in a row that every plan misses"
    level
output doctrine_length: days = military_planners.formed_length  @plan_test
    "Length of the plan that doctrine last formed around"
    level
output doctrine_counts: fraction = (1 if military_planners.doctrine is "formed" else 0)  @doctrine_time
    "Whether military plans count on cyber attacks: 1 when doctrine has formed and holds, else 0"
    level
output plan_margin: ratio = military_planners.best_chance - military_planners.acceptance  @plan_test
    "Chance that the best plan of cyber attacks meets its plan, less the chance that the planners need"
    level
    signed
output attacks_rise: fraction = (1 if leadership.attack_share > operators.share_now else 0)  @split_balance
    "Whether the leaders put more of the operators on attacks than today: 1 if they do, else 0"
    level
output new_access: fraction = 1 - effects_program.built_ahead  @waiting_cost
    "Share of attacks from access built for them on demand"
    level
output lessons_gained: targets/year = effects_program.learn_rate  @learning_basis
    "Lessons gained in a year"
    level
output lessons_lost: targets/year = effects_program.loss_rate  @forgetting
    "Lessons lost in a year"
    level
output assessment_signal: ratio = 1 / effects_program.assessment_spread  @assessment_noise
    "Signal-to-noise ratio of the yearly damage assessment"
    level
output spending: USD2025_bn/yr = (
    leadership.attack_experts + effects_program.analysis_done
) * leadership.expert_cost  @expert_cost
    "Spending on the operators and analysts of attacks in a year"
    level
output targets_hit: targets/year = effects_program.hits  @value_basis
    "Targets hit in a year"
    level
output targets_per_attack: targets/operation = effects_program.attack_targets  @targets_basis
    "Targets of one attack"
    level
output cost_per_attack: person-years/operation = effects_program.cost  @stage_cost
    "Expert work for one attack"
    level
output cost_per_target: person-years/target = effects_program.cost / effects_program.attack_targets  @stage_cost
    "Expert work for each target of an attack"
    level
output build_time: years = effects_program.lead_time  @lead_time
    "Time to build the access and the effect of one attack"
    level
output ai_share_of_work: fraction = effects_program.automation  @stage_ai
    "Share of the work of an attack that AI does"
    level
output compute_spending: USD2025_bn/yr = effects_program.compute_spending  @stage_compute
    "Spending on the compute of AI work in a year"
    level
output within_attack_correlation: fraction = effects_program.within_correlation  @outcome_correlation
    "Correlation of the outcomes on the targets of one attack"
    level
output attribution_chance: fraction = other_states.attribution  @attribution_channels
    "Chance that other countries attribute an attack"
    level
output planning_work: person-years/operation = planning_stage.learned_cost  @planning_stage
    "Expert work of target analysis and approval for one attack"
    level
output access_work: person-years/operation = access_stage.learned_cost  @access_stage
    "Expert work of access for one attack"
    level
output payload_work: person-years/operation = payload_stage.learned_cost  @payload_stage
    "Expert work of building and testing the effect for one attack"
    level
output execution_work: person-years/operation = execution_stage.learned_cost  @execution_stage
    "Expert work of running one attack"
    level
output assessment_work: person-years/operation = assessment_stage.learned_cost  @assessment_stage
    "Expert work of the damage assessment of one attack"
    level
output access_time: years = access_stage.time  @access_stage
    "Time to get access to the targets of an attack"
    level
output payload_time: years = payload_stage.time  @payload_stage
    "Time to build and test the effect of an attack"
    level
output assessment_duration: years = assessment_stage.time  @assessment_time
    "Time of the damage assessment of an attack"
    level
output access_lessons: accesses = effects_program.access_know_how  @access_lessons
    "Lessons of access that the force keeps: accesses built"
    level
output success_per_target: fraction = effects_program.success  @success_basis
    "Chance that an attack gives its intended effect on one target"
    level
output defender_attention: ratio = defenders.alertness  @alertness_balance
    "Attention of the defenders, as a loss of log-odds of an attack's success"
    level
output defender_hardening: ratio = defenders.hardening  @hardening
    "Lasting capability of the defenders, as a loss of log-odds of an attack's success"
    level
output shared_loss: fraction = day_plan.depth  @shared_depth
    "Share of a plan's effect that a failed shared capability takes"
    level
output target_kinds: capabilities/operation = effects_program.types  @target_kinds
    "Kinds of target system in one attack"
    level
output surveillance_accesses: accesses = surveillance_program.accesses_held + surveillance_program.implants  @surveillance_loop
    "Accesses that surveillance holds: those gained and the implants deployed"
    level
output accesses_used_share: fraction = surveillance_program.used / max(surveillance_program.implants,
    1 accesses)  @surveillance_use
    "Share of its implants that the collection specialists run"
    level
output accesses_burned: accesses/year = surveillance_program.burned_accesses  @capability_burn
    "Accesses of surveillance that attacks burn in a year"
    level
output surveillance_implants: accesses = surveillance_program.implants  @surveillance_loop
    "Implants that surveillance has deployed and collects from"
    level
output collected_take: access-years = surveillance_program.collected  @collection_queue
    "Take that waits for the collection specialists, in access-years"
    level
output surveillance_return: sev/person-year = surveillance_program.spying_return  @surveillance_returns
    "Return of one operator on surveillance"
    level
output analysis_served: fraction = effects_program.starts / max(effects_program.wanted_starts,
    0.001 operations/year)  @analyst_split
    "Share of the attacks that the operators can start that the analysts plan"
    level
output idle_operators: people = operators.idle  @workforce
    "Operators whose hours wait for the analysts to plan their operations"
    level
output analysts_on_attacks: fraction = analysts.attack_share  @analyst_split
    "Share of the analysts on attacks"
    level
output surveillance_planned: fraction = (
    surveillance_program.new_accesses / max(surveillance_program.builds, 0.001 accesses/year)
)  @analyst_split
    "Share of the accesses that surveillance's operators can start that the analysts plan"
    level
output research_output: capabilities/year = researchers.output  @research_yield
    "Capabilities that the researchers find in a year"
    level
output capabilities_unused: capabilities = sum(Pool.fresh)  @capability_pool
    "Unused capabilities"
    level
output capabilities_spying: capabilities = sum(Pool.spy_used)  @capability_pool
    "Capabilities that surveillance runs and that no attack has used"
    level
output capabilities_attacks: capabilities = sum(Pool.in_attacks)  @capability_pool
    "Capabilities used in an attack and not burned: held by attacks underway, or kept from earlier attacks"
    level
output capabilities_burned: capabilities = sum(Pool.burned)  @capability_burn
    "Capabilities that exposed attacks burned since the start"
output total_attacks: operations = effects_program.attacks_made  @attack_count
    "Attacks made since the start"
output deaths: people  @harm_outside
    "Deaths"
    category deaths
    unestimated "The model follows the country's cyber programs, not the harm of each attack to people."
output casualties: people  @harm_outside @hazus_severity
    "Serious casualties, including deaths"
    category casualties
    unestimated "The model follows the country's cyber programs, not the harm of each attack to people."
output economic_loss: USD2025_bn  @harm_outside
    "Economic loss"
    category loss
    unestimated "The model follows the country's cyber programs, not the loss that each attack causes."

options "Response":
    "Slow patching":
        "Defenders patch the flaws that attacks use slowly, and their attention fades within about half a
            year."
    "Fast patching":
        "Defenders patch fast, so access that the force builds in advance lasts less long, and their attention
            lasts about a year."
        effects_program.access_decay = 0.6 per_year  @patch_tiers
        defenders.calm_rate = 1 per_year  @patch_tiers
        defenders.alert_now = 0.1  @patch_tiers
        defenders.fix_rate = 15 per_year  @patch_tiers
        surveillance_program.loss_rate = 1 per_year  @patch_tiers

options "AI progress":
    "No AI progress":
        "The cyber force works as today: human experts do the tactical work."
        ai_tools.pace = 0  @no_ai_path
        effects_program.actor = "The human team"
    "Slow AI progress":
        "AI reaches about two thirds of its end in 12 years."
        ai_tools.progress_time = 12 years  @ai_pace
    "Fast AI progress":
        "AI reaches about two thirds of its end in 4 years."
        ai_tools.progress_time = 4 years  @ai_pace
    "AI progress, then costs rise":
        "AI reaches about two thirds of its end in 4 years. From 2038, the costs of attacks rise again and
            take back most of the progress."
        ai_tools.progress_time = 4 years  @ai_pace
        ai_tools.reversal_depth = 0.8  @ai_reversal

options "Analysts' AI":
    "Split AI help":
        "AI does more of the analysts' work for each target than of their work for the whole attack, where
            policy sets the review and the approval."
    "One AI share":
        "AI does one share of each kind of the analysts' work: 20% of the planning and 30% of the damage
            assessment at the end of AI progress, on the slower path."
        planning_stage.split_ai = false  @analyst_ai_options
        assessment_stage.split_ai = false  @analyst_ai_options
    "High AI help":
        "At the end of AI progress, AI does 90% of the planning and 70% of the damage assessment for each
            target, both on the faster path."
        planning_stage.target_ai_end = 0.9  @analyst_ai_options
        assessment_stage.target_ai_end = 0.7  @analyst_ai_options
        assessment_stage.target_payload_path = false  @analyst_ai_options
    "Low AI help":
        "At the end of AI progress, AI does 60% of the planning and 50% of the damage assessment for each
            target, none of the review and the approval, and 20% of the assessment of the whole attack."
        planning_stage.target_ai_end = 0.6  @analyst_ai_options
        assessment_stage.target_ai_end = 0.5  @analyst_ai_options
        planning_stage.ai_end = 0  @analyst_ai_options
        assessment_stage.ai_end = 0.2  @analyst_ai_options
    "Slow planning of surveillance":
        "AI helps the analysts with each target of an attack, but the planning of a new access of surveillance
            moves only as the review and the approval of an attack do."
        surveillance_program.plan_target_weight = 0  @surveillance_planning_ai

options "Analysts' staffing":
    "Value of the work":
        "The analysts go first to the mission where one more analyst-year is worth more."
    "Work waiting":
        "The analysts' split follows each mission's share of the analysis work that it needs."
        analysts.by_value = false  @analyst_staffing
    "Fixed split":
        "The analysts' split stays at today's."
        analysts.fixed_split = true  @analyst_staffing

options "Workforce":
    "Fixed groups":
        "The four groups of experts keep their sizes for the whole period."
    "Idle operators retrain":
        "The leaders retrain operators who wait for the analysts as analysts, at half an analyst's work, over
            about three years."
        operators.retrains = true  @workforce

options "Campaign targets":
    "One objective":
        "The force masses its attacks on one military objective, whose targets share one defender."
    "Dispersed targets":
        "The force spreads its attacks over many unconnected targets that share no defender, and the plan
            counts on their total effect."
        defenders.common_spread = 0.13  @dispersed_targets
        defenders.shared_failure = 0.03  @dispersed_targets
        defenders.failure_rate = 0.24 per_year  @dispersed_targets
        military_planners.objective_share = 1  @dispersed_targets

options "Funding rule":
    "Current results":
        "The leaders fund attacks for the value that their results give now."
    "Results and lessons":
        "The leaders also fund attacks for the value of what the attacks teach."
        leadership.foresight = 1  @funding_rules

options "Funding surge":
    "No surge":
        "The leaders get no extra money for attacks."
    "The 2025 surge":
        "The leaders get USD 0.25 billion more for attacks in each year from 2026 to 2029."
        leadership.surge_funding = 0.25 USD2025_bn/yr  @surge_2025

options "Defender hardening":
    "No hardening":
        "The defenders' lasting capability stays as it is today."
    "Slow hardening":
        "The defenders get better every year, by 0.05 log-odds of an attack's success, as more organizations
            find intrusions themselves."
        defenders.hardening_rate = 0.05 per_year  @hardening
    "Defenders' AI":
        "The defenders' AI hardens their networks: at the end of AI progress it takes 0.5 log-odds of an
            attack's success."
        defenders.ai_hardening = 0.5  @defender_ai

options "Tempo":
    "Peacetime":
        "The country is at peace."
    "Wartime":
        "The country goes to war in 2026: the moment to use an attack held ready comes about monthly, an
            attack must land within a week of the decision, and the planners review every quarter."
        military_planners.war = true  @wartime

runs:
    ai_fast = "Slow patching" + "Fast AI progress" + "Current results" + "No surge" + "One objective" + "No
        hardening" + "Peacetime"
        "Slow patching · Fast AI progress"
    no_ai = "Slow patching" + "No AI progress" + "Current results" + "No surge" + "One objective" + "No
        hardening" + "Peacetime" versus ai_fast
        "Slow patching · No AI progress"
    ai_slow = "Slow patching" + "Slow AI progress" + "Current results" + "No surge" + "One objective" + "No
        hardening" + "Peacetime"
        "Slow patching · Slow AI progress"
    ai_fast_lessons = "Slow patching" + "Fast AI progress" + "Results and lessons" + "No surge" + "One
        objective" + "No hardening" + "Peacetime"
        "Slow patching · Fast AI progress · Funding counts the lessons"
    surge_today = "Slow patching" + "No AI progress" + "Current results" + "The 2025 surge" + "One
        objective" + "No hardening" + "Peacetime"
        "Slow patching · No AI progress · The 2025 surge"
    ai_reversal = "Slow patching" + "AI progress, then costs rise" + "Current results" + "No surge" + "One
        objective" + "No hardening" + "Peacetime"
        "Slow patching · AI progress, then costs rise"
    dispersed = "Slow patching" + "Fast AI progress" + "Current results" + "No surge" + "Dispersed
        targets" + "No hardening" + "Peacetime" versus dispersed_no_ai
        "Slow patching · Fast AI progress · Dispersed targets"
    dispersed_no_ai = "Slow patching" + "No AI progress" + "Current results" + "No surge" + "Dispersed
        targets" + "No hardening" + "Peacetime"
        "Slow patching · No AI progress · Dispersed targets"
    defenders_ai = "Slow patching" + "Fast AI progress" + "Current results" + "No surge" + "One
        objective" + "Defenders' AI" + "Peacetime" versus ai_fast
        "Slow patching · Fast AI progress · Defenders' AI"
    wartime = "Slow patching" + "No AI progress" + "Current results" + "No surge" + "One objective" + "No
        hardening" + "Wartime"
        "Slow patching · No AI progress · Wartime"
    analysts_one_share = "Slow patching" + "Fast AI progress" + "One AI share" + "Current results" + "No
        surge" + "One objective" + "No hardening" + "Peacetime" versus ai_fast
        "Slow patching · Fast AI progress · One AI share for each stage of the analysts"
    slow_spy_planning = "Slow patching" + "Fast AI progress" + "Slow planning of surveillance" + "Current
        results" + "No surge" + "One objective" + "No hardening" + "Peacetime" versus ai_fast
        "Slow patching · Fast AI progress · Slow planning of surveillance"

notes:
    premises:
        START: "The model starts in 2026 with today's split of the experts. Today's split is a rest point of
            the funding rule, so without AI the force stays where it starts. The numbers of today come from
            the United States, the one country with a public split of money and people."
    limitations:
        one-country: "The model follows one country. Other countries' attacks, and a race between countries,
            change the attention of defenders and the value of attacks in ways that the model does not
            follow."  @program_basis
        value-unit: "Value is in units of one expert-year of surveillance in 2026. The model compares the two
            missions; it gives no price of either."  @sev_definition
        assumed-ranges: "Most values are assumptions of this scenario, with ranges that a reader can explore.
            A range is not a measured interval."  @program_basis
    decisive: "Whether the lessons of attacks outlast their loss to patches, turnover and secrecy at the
        volume that cheaper attacks allow."

record research_basis: assumption A-SC45-CAPABILITIES "Researchers find flaws that the defenders do not know
    and build the tools that use them. One group serves surveillance and attacks, and its work goes to each
    pool as the pool needs it."
record research_share: assumption A-SC45-EXPERTS "Three in a hundred of the country's experts are researchers.
    No count is public, so the scenario chooses the share. Today's work of the researchers meets today's
    needs, so the results depend on its ratios."
record capability_reserve: assumption A-SC45-CAPABILITIES "Each pool keeps never-used capabilities for a year
    of the missions' draws from it. When a pool's never-used stock falls below about a week of its draws, the
    parts of each step that need a new zero-day slow down with it, and the missions start only the operations
    that they can equip. The scenario chooses both times."
record research_ai: assumption A-SC45-AI "AI finds flaws and builds tools. At the end of AI progress, a
    researcher finds ten times as many capabilities in a year as today. One model found and exploited
    zero-days in every major operating system and browser. Automated systems found 86% of the synthetic flaws
    of one contest. The scenario chooses the multiple."
    claims:
        E-SC45-MYTHOS-FLAWS
        E-SC45-AIXCC
record research_split: assumption A-SC45-CAPABILITIES "Each pool asks the researchers for the never-used
    capabilities that the missions take from it and that the defenders find, and for what restores its
    reserve. The researchers do no more than the pools ask, while they can. So each never-used stock stays
    near its reserve, and today's pools are at rest."
record research_yield: identity "Today a researcher finds the needs of all of the pools over the researchers
    in a year. So the work of the researchers meets today's needs."
record operator_basis: assumption A-SC45-EXPERTS "Operators do the hands-on work of operations. On attacks
    they gain access, move inside the network, prepare the effect and execute it. On surveillance they gain
    access, move inside the network and deploy implants. The leaders move operators between the missions."
record operator_priority: assumption A-SC45-EXPERTS "The operators' hours go to the operations farthest along
    first. On attacks, execution comes first, then the effect, then movement inside the network, then initial
    access. Each step runs at the lower of its pace by time and the pace that its hours allow. So the force
    finishes the attacks underway before it starts new ones."
record expert_pools: identity "The groups of experts are fixed by default, and they add up to the experts.
    Today's experts on attacks are operators and analysts as the stages of an attack say. The researchers are
    part of the agency's experts. Its other experts are operators who deploy implants, analysts who plan new
    accesses, and collection specialists, in shares. So today's work sets the size of each group, and today's
    share of the operators on attacks follows from it."
record workforce: assumption A-SC45-EXPERTS "By default the four groups of experts keep their sizes for the
    whole period, so operators whose operations the analysts cannot plan wait, and the model reports them. As
    an option, the leaders retrain those operators as analysts: about two thirds of them move in three years,
    and each does half an analyst's work. No operator waits today, so today stays at rest. The scenario
    chooses these values; no source measures them."
record collection_basis: assumption A-SC45-SURVEILLANCE "Collection specialists run collection on
    surveillance's deployed implants, translate, and turn the raw take into intelligence. They are the
    surveillance experts who neither deploy implants nor plan new accesses. In 2011 the implant program made
    full use of only an eighth of its machines with implants."
    claims:
        E-SC45-GENIE-2011
record analyst_basis: assumption A-SC45-EXPERTS "Analysts plan the operations of both missions, assess the
    damage of attacks and use the intelligence. Their split between the missions moves toward each mission's
    share of the analysis work waiting. On attacks they assess the damage of the attacks made first, then plan
    new ones. So a mission whose work grows takes analysts from the other."
record analyst_split: assumption A-SC45-EXPERTS "The analysts' split moves toward the target of the staffing
    rule, about two thirds of the way in one year. On attacks, damage assessment takes at most its share of
    the analysis work waiting, and planning takes the rest. Today's split is a rest point of every staffing
    rule. The scenario chooses the time."
record analyst_staffing: assumption A-SC45-EXPERTS "The leaders staff the analysts by the value of their work.
    Each mission gets its analysis work first in proportion to the priority that the value of one more
    analyst-year gives it, and the split moves toward that target over the adjustment time. One more analyst-
    year is worth much more on attacks than on surveillance's planning, because the collection specialists,
    not the accesses, limit surveillance's take. The options keep the split by the work that each mission
    needs, or fixed at today's. Each rule gives today's split at rest. The scenario chooses the rule; no
    source measures it."
record analyst_load: assumption A-SC45-EXPERTS "Today's work fills 90% of the capacity of the analysts. So a
    small growth of the work does not slow it, and a large growth does. The scenario chooses the share."
record surveillance_analysts: assumption A-SC45-EXPERTS "Half of the surveillance experts are analysts, and a
    tenth of them plan new accesses. The rest of them process what the accesses collect, as collection
    specialists. The scenario chooses both shares."
record surveillance_planning_ai: assumption A-SC45-AI "To plan a new access of surveillance is analysis of one
    target, so by default AI helps it as fully as it helps the analysis of each target of an attack. With a
    share of 0, its planning moves only as the review and the approval of an attack do. No source measures the
    planning work of an access, so the model reports results across the share."
record analysis_ai: assumption A-SC45-AI "AI does 10% of the collection specialists' processing of the take
    today and 60% at the end of AI progress, on the slower path of AI progress: translation and triage. AI did
    most of the work of one espionage campaign, and that work included the sorting of the stolen data. The
    scenario chooses the shares."
    claims:
        E-SC45-AI-CAMPAIGN
record capability_burn: assumption A-SC45-CAPABILITIES "An exposed attack burns the capabilities that it
    holds, and an attack that stays hidden keeps them for reuse. In a year the force makes the same share of
    the attacks that hold capabilities of a pool, so that share of the held capabilities leaves the pool's
    held stocks. Where surveillance also uses a burned capability, the defenders find 2% of the accesses that
    run on it. The leaders count each access that the defenders find at the value that one access gives in its
    life today. The scenario chooses the share."
record capability_pool: assumption A-SC45-CAPABILITIES "A capability is a flaw that the defenders do not know,
    with the tools that use it, and it burns as a whole. Each step has a pool: access and movement in the
    network serve both missions, the effect serves attacks and collection serves surveillance. A capability is
    never used, used by surveillance, used in an attack, or burned. Used in an attack, it is held by an attack
    underway, or kept from an earlier attack that was not exposed."
record one_day_chance: assumption A-SC45-CAPABILITIES "A need of a step takes no capability when a known flaw
    or the target's own functions serve it, on a target that has not fixed the flaw by its use. They can serve
    80% of the needs of access and of the effect, and 90% of movement and collection. Industrial systems fix
    flaws at a fifth of the pace, and the systems inside a network at half. The scenario chooses the values."
    claims:
        E-SC45-KEV-FIX
        E-SC45-KEV-2025
        E-SC45-DBIR-SATURATION
record capability_use: assumption A-SC45-CAPABILITIES "A step takes one capability for each kind of target
    system that no known flaw serves: one for each kind of system of an attack, and one for each access of
    surveillance. Each such zero-day is a capability already in use or a never-used one."
record capability_sources: assumption A-SC45-CAPABILITIES "Today a tenth of an attack's zero-days are
    capabilities that surveillance already uses, and a twentieth reuse a capability of an earlier attack that
    was not burned. Surveillance serves 95% of its zero-day needs with capabilities that it already uses, and
    one need in 10,000 with a capability of an earlier attack. A capability in use serves a need as one
    independent try: the chance is one minus the exponential of minus the tries, and the tries grow in
    proportion to the capabilities in use. The rest of the needs take never-used capabilities. No count is
    public. The scenario chooses the shares."
record capability_rest: identity "Today each pool is at rest: each stock gains in a year the capabilities that
    it loses. So today's stocks follow from today's needs and shares, from the pace at which the force makes
    the attacks that hold them, and from the rate at which others find flaws. The checks reject shares that no
    rest point can keep."
record target_kinds: assumption A-SC45-CAPABILITIES "A further target of an attack runs a kind of system that
    the other targets do not with a chance of 10%. So an attack on 30 targets needs about four capabilities at
    a step, not 30. The scenario chooses the chance."
record ai_path: assumption A-SC45-AI "AI progress reaches the cyber force on two paths: one for access, the
    running of attacks, the running of implants and the defenders, and a slower one for payloads, target
    analysis, damage assessment, the planning of new accesses and the processing of the take. Each is 1 -
    exp(-t / its time), times the pace. The reversal option takes back a share of both after a start time."
record ai_pace: assumption A-SC45-AI "The scenario assumes how fast AI progress reaches the cyber force. The
    default reaches about two thirds of the end values in 5 years; the options give 4 and 12 years."
record analyst_ai_options: assumption A-SC45-AI "The options test the analysts' AI shares. One AI share is the
    earlier form: one share for each stage of the analysts. High and low help take the ends of the ranges of
    the split: 90% and 70%, or 60% and 50%, of the work for each target at the end. In the low option AI does
    none of the review and the approval, and 20% of the assessment of the whole attack. Today is the same in
    every option."
record ai_reversal: assumption A-SC45-AI "The reversal option tests hysteresis: the costs of attacks rise
    again after 12 years and take back 80% of AI progress. The scenario chooses these values to test whether
    the force stays where it got to; no source predicts them."
record no_ai_path: assumption A-SC45-AI "Without AI progress, every driver keeps its value of today: the run
    is the matched human pathway of the scenario."
record split_basis: assumption A-SC45-SPLIT "The leaders split one pool of operators between the two missions.
    Each operator on attacks is an operator taken from surveillance."
    claims:
        E-SC45-CARTER-ISIS
record experts_pool: assumption A-SC45-SPLIT "The pool is the NSA workforce and the posts of Cyber Command's
    attack-oriented teams as designed. The NSA had about 39,000 people in 2024 and shed about 2,000 in 2025,
    so the pool is 37,000 + 3,535 = 40,535 people: researchers, operators, collection specialists and
    analysts. Not all of them are cyber experts, so the pool sets the scale of the model, and the results
    depend on ratios."
    claims:
        E-SC45-NSA-WORKFORCE
        E-SC45-CMF-SIZE
record share_today: assumption A-SC45-SPLIT "Today's share of the experts on attacks is the share of Cyber
    Command's attack-oriented posts in the pool: 3,535 / 40,535 = 0.087, a staff ratio of about 10.5:1. The
    range covers the ratios of staff (6.3:1 to 12.7:1) and money (5.4:1 to 8.9:1) in the workbook's sources."
    claims:
        E-SC45-NSA-WORKFORCE
        E-SC45-CMF-SIZE
        E-SC45-CYBERCOM-BUDGET
record split_limits: assumption A-SC45-SPLIT "The leaders keep a floor of operators on attacks, for strategy
    and readiness, whatever the results, and they never put more than a ceiling on attacks. Today about 58% of
    the operators who do the hands-on work of operations are on attacks, so the ceiling is 90%. The scenario
    chooses both."
record funding_speed: assumption A-SC45-SPLIT "Budgets and teams follow results slowly. The defense budget
    process starts more than two years before the money is spent, and trained teams take years to build. The
    scenario takes three years for the split to move about two thirds of the way to its target, with a range
    from one to six years."
    claims:
        E-SC45-PPBE
record funding_response: assumption A-SC45-SPLIT "The target split is a logistic function of the difference in
    returns between attacks and surveillance, between the floor and the ceiling. Equal returns keep today's
    split. The steepness says how strongly budgets follow results. In one study of US programs, 10 points on a
    100-point performance score moved proposed budgets by 4 to 5%. If those 10 points stand for a difference
    in returns of 0.02 to 0.1, the steepness is about 0.8 to 4. The scenario takes 2, with a range from 0.5 to
    10."
    claims:
        E-SC45-PART-BUDGET
record funding_forms: assumption A-SC45-SPLIT "The proportional form is the second form of the funding rule:
    the split moves at a rate proportional to the difference in returns, within its floor and ceiling, as in
    replicator dynamics."
record funding_rules: assumption A-SC45-SPLIT "Funding compares the assessed return of an operator on attacks
    with the return of an operator on surveillance, as a share of surveillance's current return. With
    foresight, it adds the value of the lessons of the operator's attacks."
record discount_rate: assumption A-SC45-SPLIT "The scenario assumes a discount rate of 3% a year for the
    future value of lessons, with a range from 2% to 10%."
record budget_cycle: assumption A-SC45-SPLIT "The leaders assess the results once a year, as a yearly budget
    does."
record split_balance: identity "The operators on attacks and on surveillance add up to the operators. The
    share on attacks moves by two flows: the flow that raises it and the flow that lowers it."
record revealed_split: assumption A-SC45-SPLIT "Today's split is a rest point of the funding rule, where an
    operator on attacks and an operator on surveillance return the same. The value of one intended effect
    follows from it: the value that makes today's returns equal. With foresight, the return includes the value
    of lessons. So the model calibrates value by the revealed choice of the country, not by the claims of the
    essay."
record sev_definition: identity "The unit of value is the yearly value of one expert on surveillance in 2026."
record surge_basis: assumption A-SC45-SURGE "A one-off surge of money buys extra experts for attacks while it
    lasts, at the cost of one expert-year. The model treats the money as extra capacity, not as money taken
    from surveillance."
record surge_2025: assumption A-SC45-SURGE "The 2025 law sets aside USD 1 billion for offensive cyber
    operations, available through 2029: USD 0.25 billion a year for four years."
    claims:
        E-SC45-SURGE-2025
record expert_cost: assumption A-SC45-SURGE "The scenario assumes USD 0.42 million for one expert-year: the
    resources of Cyber Command in FY2026, USD 2.6 billion, over the 6,187 people of the Cyber Mission Force."
record assessment_noise: assumption A-SC45-NOISE "A yearly assessment of the value of attacks has an error in
    log units. One part, 0.8 for one outcome, falls with the targets hit and with the assessed outcomes, as an
    average does. The central run takes the median error, 0."
record assessment_common: assumption A-SC45-NOISE "Part of the error of a yearly assessment is common to all
    of its outcomes, so it does not fall with their number: 0.3 log units. In the 1991 air campaign the
    services and their contractors shared one error: the claimed hit rate of one bomber was 80% against 41% to
    60% in a later audit. The scenario chooses the value."
    claims:
        E-SC45-DESERT-STORM-BDA
record assessment_bias: assumption A-SC45-NOISE "As an option, the bias of the assessment changes along the AI
    path: an AI that writes assessments can overstate, as one AI-orchestrated campaign made up credentials and
    claimed secret data that was public. A bias that does not change only rescales the value of an effect that
    today's split shows, so by default the bias does not change."
    claims:
        E-SC45-AI-CAMPAIGN
record wartime: assumption A-SC45-PLANNING "As an option, the country goes to war in 2026 from today's
    peacetime posture. In war the moment to use an attack held ready comes about monthly, an attack must land
    within a week of the decision, and the planners review every quarter. In 2022 Russia massed its
    destructive attacks at the war's outset and then spent its first stockpiles: 22 organizations were hit in
    the first week, about one a week from mid-April, and the tempo lasted only weeks. The scenario chooses the
    values."
    claims:
        E-SC45-WARTIME-TEMPO
record surveillance_basis: assumption A-SC45-SURVEILLANCE "Surveillance gains accesses in other countries'
    networks with capabilities from the pools, moves inside them and deploys implants. It gets value each year
    from the take that its collection specialists process, less the loss from alert defenders. It loses
    accesses to the defenders and to exposed attacks, and its operators gain new ones."
record surveillance_accesses: assumption A-SC45-SURVEILLANCE "Surveillance holds 85,000 accesses today:
    accesses gained and implants deployed on computers and networks. The agency had 68,975 machines with
    active implants in 2011 and had deployed between 85,000 and 100,000 by 2014, from 100 to 150 in 2004. No
    later count is public, so the scenario takes the count of 2014 for today. The results depend on its
    ratios."
    claims:
        E-SC45-IMPLANT-COUNT
        E-SC45-GENIE-2011
record surveillance_use: assumption A-SC45-SURVEILLANCE "The collection specialists run only some of the
    implants, because people limit exploitation at a large scale. In 2011 the implant program made full use of
    8,448 of its 68,975 machines, 12%, and the scenario takes that share for today. The implants run are the
    implants times the reach of the collection specialists over their sum, so they are always fewer than
    either."
    claims:
        E-SC45-TURBINE
        E-SC45-GENIE-2011
record collection_queue: assumption A-SC45-SURVEILLANCE "The take of the implants waits in a queue until the
    collection specialists process it, about five weeks today. They process the take first and run collection
    with the hours left, so a backlog takes hours from collection. One implant-year of collection gives one
    access-year of take. No time is public. The scenario chooses it."
record surveillance_builders: assumption A-SC45-SURVEILLANCE "A twentieth of the surveillance experts are
    operators who gain access and deploy and keep implants: the implant program had 1,870 staff in 2011,
    against a pool of about 37,000 experts today. The work of a new access is its initial access and the
    deployment that it will need, for the accesses that last until their deployment."
    claims:
        E-SC45-GENIE-2011
record surveillance_builds: identity "Today's new accesses replace the accesses that surveillance loses, so
    today's accesses are a rest point. That sets the operators' work to gain one access and deploy its implant
    today. The defenders also find a few accesses through burned capabilities, so surveillance holds a little
    fewer accesses at rest than the builds alone keep. The work then moves with AI as the access work of an
    attack does, because it is the same work. The analysts plan the new accesses, so surveillance starts fewer
    when the analysts cannot plan all of them."
record surveillance_priority: assumption A-SC45-EXPERTS "The operators on surveillance deploy implants on the
    accesses that they have gained first, and gain new initial access with the hours left. Initial access and
    deployment split the operators' work to gain one access and deploy its implant as the access of an attack
    splits, and they take the same times."
record surveillance_loss: assumption A-SC45-SURVEILLANCE "Surveillance loses its accesses at 50% a year to the
    defenders, to patches and to changes. Espionage hides long: in the intrusions that one firm investigated
    in 2025, espionage stayed a median of 122 days before it was found, against 14 days for all intrusions,
    and one backdoor stayed nearly 400 days. Accesses that no one finds last longer. The scenario chooses the
    rate."
    claims:
        E-SC45-ESPIONAGE-DWELL
record surveillance_loop: identity "An access moves from initial access to held, and becomes an implant when
    surveillance has moved inside the network and deployed it. Accesses held and implants leave when
    surveillance loses them or the defenders find them through burned capabilities. At rest, each stock is
    what enters it in a year over the rate at which it leaves."
record surveillance_returns: assumption A-SC45-SURVEILLANCE "The value of surveillance grows as the take that
    the collection specialists process, to the power 0.8: each extra access adds less. They process what the
    implants that they run collect, as far as they can. Today the value is one expert-year of surveillance for
    each expert. The scenario chooses the exponent."
record surveillance_alert: assumption A-SC45-SURVEILLANCE "Alert defenders lower the value of surveillance
    less than the value of attacks, because surveillance stays hidden. The scenario chooses the factor."
record surveillance_ai: assumption A-SC45-SURVEILLANCE "AI does a growing share of the work of running
    implants: 30% today, because the automated control of implants in groups has run since 2010, and 90% at
    the end of AI progress, as in one AI-orchestrated espionage campaign. AI work costs supervision, which the
    implants that one collection specialist runs at once share, and compute, as in the stages of an attack. So
    the gain of surveillance from AI is a result, and the implants deployed limit it."
    claims:
        E-SC45-TURBINE
        E-SC45-AI-CAMPAIGN
record defender_basis: assumption A-SC45-DEFENDERS "The defenders adapt in three parts. Their attention
    follows the threat flow that they answer, of which the force's attacks are a small share, saturates and
    fades within months; it lowers the log-odds of an attack's success and the value of surveillance. They fix
    what an attack exposes, so an attack that reuses it may fail. As an option, their lasting capability grows
    with time and with their own AI."
record threat_flow: assumption A-SC45-DEFENDERS "The defenders answer the whole threat flow: 884 flaws were
    first seen exploited in 2025, and 90 zero-days were exploited in the wild, from many actors. The force's
    attacks are 5% of the threat that they answer today, counted in targets, and the rest of the flow stays as
    it is. The scenario chooses the share."
    claims:
        E-SC45-KEV-2025
        E-SC45-ZERODAY-2025
record attention_cap: assumption A-SC45-DEFENDERS "Attention saturates. When the flaws in one dataset grew
    from 69 million to 527 million in three years, the median time to fix an exploited flaw rose from 32 to 43
    days in a year, the share fully fixed fell from 38% to 26%, and 60% to 70% stayed open after a week
    whatever the maturity of the organization. So attention rises with the threat flow towards a cap, six
    times today's, 0.3 log-odds: no data show a larger drop. The scenario chooses the cap."
    claims:
        E-SC45-DBIR-SATURATION
record alertness_today: assumption A-SC45-DEFENDERS "Today's attention is a rest point: today's threat flow
    draws the defenders to today's attention, 0.05 log-odds. No study measures it; values much above 0.1 would
    imply drops in success at high attack rates that no data show."
record alertness_fades: assumption A-SC45-DEFENDERS "Attention fades at 2 a year. Two actors that a security
    firm exposed returned to their earlier activity after about five months, and quickly moved to new
    infrastructure. The scenario chooses the rate."
    claims:
        E-SC45-APT1-RETURN
record alertness_balance: identity "Attention moves towards the level that the threat flow draws, at the rate
    at which it fades: one flow raises it and the other lowers it."
record outcome_correlation: assumption A-SC45-DEFENDERS "The targets of one attack share steps: one access
    path, one tool, one command. When a shared step works, each target still succeeds or fails on its own;
    when it fails, all fail together. Today the correlation is 0.85: in documented attacks, the targets that
    shared the decisive step fell together. A share of the log-chance of success lies in the shared steps, so
    the correlation follows from it. Lessons do not change the shared share, so the correlation stays at
    today's chance of success: an approximation that keeps the analysis of rest points simple."
    claims:
        V-C01
record common_factor: assumption A-SC45-DEFENDERS "Attacks massed on one objective face one defender, its
    national CERT and a few security vendors. So the log-odds of a plan's success move together around the
    plan: by 0.3 by default, with a range from 0.1 to 0.46. Against one defender, the monthly hit share of a
    drone campaign moved by about 0.27 to 0.46 in log-odds; unconnected victims of ransomware moved together
    by at most about 0.13 a quarter. All of those drones were one type of weapon, so the drone figure can also
    hold a part from the attacker's side. The steps that the attacks themselves share are a separate step that
    can fail."
    claims:
        E-SC45-SHAHED-2026
record shared_failure: assumption A-SC45-DEFENDERS "A capability that the attacks of a plan share, such as one
    tool, one access path or one channel of command, can fail for all of the attacks that use it at once: at
    the start of the plan with a chance of 5%, and while it runs at a rate of 0.6 a year, as the defenders
    find it. Each kind of target system needs its own such capability, and each fails on its own with today's
    chance, so the number of failures in a plan is Poisson. Each failure takes its share of the plan. Such
    failures are all or nothing, so the model counts them as a mixture over their number and not as a
    correlation. The scenario chooses the values."
record shared_depth: assumption A-SC45-PLANNING "A failed shared capability takes the attacks that depend on
    it, and they give no effect. Each kind of target system in an attack needs its own capability. So one
    failure takes a smaller share of a plan as the kinds of target system grow, but more capabilities can
    fail: each kind's capability fails on its own. The scenario assumes that one failure takes half of today's
    plan, which has one kind."
record plan_concentration: assumption A-SC45-DEFENDERS "Today half of a plan's effect depends on one
    capability that its attacks share: half of today's attacks reuse a flaw, tool or infrastructure that an
    earlier attack exposed. One state actor built a new variant of its 2016 tool from the same source code,
    and at least nine of 18 zero-days exploited in early 2022 were variants of patched flaws. The scenario
    chooses the share."
    claims:
        E-SC45-INDUSTROYER2
        E-SC45-ZERODAY-VARIANTS
record dispersed_targets: assumption A-SC45-DEFENDERS "Unconnected targets share no defender, so their
    log-odds move together by about 0.13, the bound that ransomware victims show, and a shared step fails less
    often. The plan counts on the total effect of all of the force's attacks. The option tests the essay's
    claim that AI makes many small, unconnected attacks reliable in total. Across campaigns, the success of
    mass attacks on unconnected victims through one flaw still fell as the victims' defenses matured."
    claims:
        E-SC45-COVEWARE-CAMPAIGNS
record patch_tiers: assumption A-SC45-DEFENDERS "The fast defenders lose the force's advance access and
    surveillance's accesses twice as fast, keep their attention twice as long and start with twice the
    attention, and they fix a known flaw at 15 a year, the pace at which most servers fixed one famous flaw
    within three weeks. The scenario chooses these values."
    claims:
        E-SC45-PROXYLOGON-FIX
record rediscovery: assumption A-SC45-DEFENDERS "AI helps the defenders find and close flaws: in every run,
    discovery and patching rise with AI progress, to three times today's rates at the end. In one competition,
    AI systems found 86% of the synthetic flaws and patched 68% of those. The scenario chooses the growth."
    claims:
        E-SC45-AIXCC
record loss_routes: assumption A-SC45-CAPABILITIES "A flaw that the force holds is lost in three ways: others
    find it on their own and disclose it, a patch or code change made for another reason closes it, or its
    systems leave service or stop mattering. For a stockpiled flaw the three together give the 14.5% a year of
    a 6.9-year life. Independent rediscovery is 5.7% a year of it, and the scenario splits the rest evenly
    between patching and irrelevance, because the study gives no split. A flaw in use can also be seen at
    work: zero-days used in attacks lasted about 312 days before their disclosure, so the scenario takes 1.17
    a year for a flaw in use, an upper bracket for a careful force. AI speeds discovery and patching in every
    run, up to three times by the end of AI progress; irrelevance does not change."
    claims:
        E-SC45-ZERODAY-LIFE
        E-SC45-ZD-LONGEVITY
        E-SC45-ZD-COLLISION
        E-SC45-ZD-LIFE-STATUS
        E-SC45-ZD-IN-USE-LIFE
record hardening: assumption A-SC45-DEFENDERS "As an option, the defenders' lasting capability grows by 0.05
    log-odds of an attack's success a year: organizations first found the intrusions themselves in 52% of one
    firm's investigations of 2025, up from 43% in 2024, while the median dwell rose from 11 to 14 days. The
    default has none, so the run without AI stays at rest. The scenario chooses the rate."
    claims:
        E-SC45-INTERNAL-DETECTION
        E-SC45-ESPIONAGE-DWELL
record defender_ai: assumption A-SC45-DEFENDERS "As an option, the defenders' AI hardens their networks: it
    takes 0.5 log-odds of an attack's success at the end of AI progress. AI systems found 86% of the synthetic
    flaws of one competition and patched 68% of those, in 45 minutes on average at about USD 152 a task, and
    one model found thousands of serious flaws. Fixes across organizations have not sped up, so the default
    has no hardening. AI's help to find and close flaws is in every run."
    claims:
        E-SC45-AIXCC
        E-SC45-MYTHOS-FLAWS
record politics_basis: assumption A-SC45-POLITICS "An attributed attack draws sanctions or replies. The
    expected cost of one attack is the chance of attribution times the cost of an attributed attack."
    claims:
        E-SC45-NOTPETYA-ATTRIBUTION
        E-SC45-NOTPETYA-SANCTIONS
record attribution_channels: assumption A-SC45-POLITICS "Other countries attribute an attack by four kinds of
    evidence: its code and tools, its infrastructure, its operators' habits, and its choice of targets and
    time. An attack stays unattributed only when every kind misses, so the chance of attribution is one less
    the product of the misses: 0.73 today. Technical attribution of significant attacks runs about 0.6 to
    0.95, and every large destructive state attack of recent years was attributed."
    claims:
        E-SC45-NOTPETYA-ATTRIBUTION
        E-SC45-POLAND-2025
record tools_channel: assumption A-SC45-AI "AI writes new code for each attack, so the code and tools of an
    attack attribute it far less often: 40% today, 5% at the end of AI progress. In the Polish attacks of
    2025, a wiper that AI may have written could not be used for attribution, while the attack's
    infrastructure still overlapped with a known cluster."
    claims:
        E-SC45-POLAND-2025
record infrastructure_channel: assumption A-SC45-POLITICS "The servers and accounts of an attack attribute it
    30% of the time today, and AI does not change that. The operators' habits and the choice of targets each
    attribute it 20% of the time."
record sanction_cost: assumption A-SC45-POLITICS "The scenario assumes the cost of the sanctions and replies
    to one attributed attack: 10 expert-years of surveillance value."
    claims:
        E-SC45-NOTPETYA-SANCTIONS
record escalation: assumption A-SC45-POLITICS "With escalation, the cost of sanctions grows with the
    attributed attacks in a year. The default has none. Formal replies to cyber attacks are few, and an
    escalation is more a threshold than a steady rise. So the range stops at 0.01 years for each attack: at
    today's tempo of about 260 attributed attacks a year, that multiplies the cost of each attributed attack
    by about 3.6."
record planning_basis: assumption A-SC45-PLANNING "Planners build plans around cyber attacks only when a plan
    of attacks meets its plan reliably: when the chance that it hits the planned share of targets, less a
    tolerance, passes a level that the planners accept. One use of an attack is not doctrine: doctrine needs
    reviews that pass again and again."
record plan_test: assumption A-SC45-PLANNING "A plan counts on the attacks that the force can mass on one
    objective in its time. The planners test plans of four lengths, from one day to one quarter, by one rule,
    and a review passes when the best of them meets its plan often enough. So the model does not choose what
    doctrine becomes: the length that doctrine forms around is a result."
record plan_lengths: assumption A-SC45-PLANNING "The plans run for one day, one week, one month and one
    quarter: from a salvo that opens an operation to a long campaign. The scenario chooses the lengths."
record held_capabilities: identity "The attacks that the force holds ready are a stock, and each keeps the
    targets, kinds of target system and correlation that it was built with. An attack built in advance goes
    into the stock when it is ready, and leaves it when its moment comes. At rest the stock is the attacks of
    a year that need access built in advance, times their mean wait. A plan can use these attacks and the
    attacks that the force finishes in the plan's time and that the pools can equip."
record objective_share: assumption A-SC45-PLANNING "One objective can draw on a share of the force's attacks,
    because the force serves several commands and adversaries. The scenario chooses 20%, with a range from 5%
    to all of them."
record forecast_error: assumption A-SC45-PLANNING "The planners do not know the share of targets that a plan
    will hit. Their error has a floor that lessons do not remove, 10% by default and up to 30%, and a part
    that the force's assessed outcomes reduce: the sampling error of the chance of success from those
    outcomes, not from the prior. After the 1991 air campaign, the claimed hit rate of one bomber was 80%,
    against 41 to 60% that a later audit found."
    claims:
        E-SC45-DESERT-STORM-BDA
record plan_tolerance: assumption A-SC45-PLANNING "The planners accept a shortfall of 25% from the planned
    share of targets hit, and need a chance of 80% to meet the plan. Planners guard against a shortfall with
    redundancy, and an effect above the plan does not break it. The evidence brackets the ranges: a chance of
    75% to 95%, higher for effects on which other parts of an operation depend, and a shortfall of 20% to 50%.
    As an option, the test of the earlier model also fails a plan that hits more than the tolerance above it."
record plan_spread: assumption A-SC45-PLANNING "The share of targets that a plan hits varies with the luck of
    each target, the shared causes of the targets of one attack, the common factor of the plan and the
    planners' error. The planned share is the mean share, because the planners learn it from pooled outcomes.
    The chance to meet the plan is a normal probability of the sum, mixed over the number of shared
    capabilities that fail. After a failure, only the luck of the attacks that are left counts."
record doctrine_time: assumption A-SC45-PLANNING "Military plans count on cyber attacks after three planning
    reviews in a row pass, with a range from 1 to 10. The planners review once a year in peace. Reliance on a
    new weapon took one to two years in war and decades in peace."
record doctrine_lapse: assumption A-SC45-PLANNING "Doctrine lapses after two reviews in a row in which every
    plan misses, with a range from 1 to 6. A guided shell whose hits fell from more than 50% to under 10%
    under jamming lost its place in plans within months. Doctrine can form again after more passes."
    claims:
        E-SC45-EXCALIBUR
record doctrine_feedback: assumption A-SC45-PLANNING "By default doctrine changes no value: it only shows when
    plans count on attacks. The option of a doctrine value tests a loop in which doctrine raises the value of
    effects."
record program_basis: assumption A-SC45-STRUCTURE "One country with four groups of experts and four pools of
    capabilities. The amounts that change are the split of the operators and the split of the analysts; five
    stocks of an attack's targets and of attacks, from initial access to damage assessment; and four stocks of
    surveillance, from initial access to the take that waits to be processed. They are also six stocks of each
    pool of capabilities, two stocks of lessons, of effects and of access, and the attention of the defenders.
    The drivers of AI change over time."
record cost_today: identity "Today's work of one attack is the sum of the work of its stages today. The sum is
    a check, not a target: today's 3,535 attack posts over about 231 operations a year give 15 person-years,
    and the staff of the access program over the same operations give about 8."
    claims:
        E-SC45-OPS-2011
        E-SC45-CMF-SIZE
        E-SC45-GENIE-2011
record stage_basis: assumption A-SC45-COSTS "An attack needs five kinds of work: target analysis and approval,
    access to the targets, building and testing the effect, running the attack, and damage assessment. Access
    is two steps: initial access and movement inside the network. Each kind has work for the attack and work
    for each target, a share that AI does and a time. The cost of an attack, the time to build it and AI's
    share of its work follow from them. Analysts do the target analysis and the damage assessment, and
    operators do the rest."
record stage_work: assumption A-SC45-COSTS "Each kind of work has expert work by hand for the attack and for
    each of its targets. The scenario sets the values from public anchors, stage by stage. Their sum today is
    a check against the evidence, not a target."
record stage_cost: identity "The work of a stage for one attack is its work by hand times its cost share, and
    times the cost of waiting when its product waits ready for use."
record stage_ai: assumption A-SC45-AI "AI does a share of each kind of work. A unit of AI work still costs an
    expert's supervision, which the attacks that one expert runs at once share, and the compute that it uses.
    So supervising attacks in parallel saves only the work that AI does."
record stage_paths: assumption A-SC45-AI "AI progress reaches access, the running of attacks and the analysis
    of each target faster than the building of payloads, the review and the approval of an attack and damage
    assessment. AI has done most of the tactical work of an espionage campaign, while the most complex
    intrusions and attacks on industrial systems stay mostly manual. The slower path reaches about two thirds
    of its end in 10 years by default."
record stage_split: assumption A-SC45-AI "The analysts' work has two parts. The work for each target is the
    analysis of the target and the check of the effect on it. The work for the whole attack is the legal
    review, the approval and the assessment of the whole effect. AI can do more of the work for each target,
    and sooner, while policy sets how much of the review and the approval AI does: in kinetic targeting, AI
    cut the staff of target work by up to a hundred times, but approval still needs human judgment, and its
    time changed only by delegation. So each stage of the analysts has one AI share for each part, and each
    part has its own AI path. The scenario chooses this split; the operators' stages keep one share."
    claims:
        E-SC45-WF-MAVEN-EXERCISE
        E-SC45-AN-HUMAN-JUDGMENT
        E-SC45-AN-CIA-APPROVAL
record stage_supervision: source_transfer A-SC45-AI "In one AI-orchestrated espionage campaign, AI did 80% to
    90% of the tactical work against about 30 targets, and humans decided at a few points. So the scenario
    takes 0.2 expert-years of supervision for each expert-year of AI work when an expert runs one attack at a
    time, and ten attacks at once at the end of AI progress."
    claims:
        E-SC45-AI-CAMPAIGN
record stage_compute: assumption A-SC45-AI "An expert-year is about 2,000 working hours. One AI-built exploit
    chain that experts said would take them weeks cost under USD 2,000 of compute: about USD 25 for each
    expert-hour of 80, or USD 0.05 million for an expert-year. A 20-hour attack range took a budget of 100
    million tokens, and the model solved it in 3 of 10 tries, so a finished task costs about three times one
    try. The scenario takes USD 0.1 million for each expert-year of AI work today, and a tenth of that at the
    end of AI progress."
    claims:
        E-SC45-OP-MYTHOS-COST
        E-SC45-AISI-RANGE
record planning_stage: assumption A-SC45-COSTS "Target analysis, legal review and approval take 1 expert-year
    for an attack and half an expert-year for each target. Approval of one operation has taken a series of
    meetings at the top of the government. Policy, not AI, sets the pace of the review and the approval: a
    delegation of 2018 cut the approval of cyber operations from as much as a year to weeks, and US policy
    keeps human judgment over the use of force. So AI does none of the work for the attack today and 20% at
    the end."
    claims:
        E-SC45-GLOWING-SYMPHONY
        E-SC45-AN-CIA-APPROVAL
        E-SC45-AN-NSPM13-SPEED
        E-SC45-AN-HUMAN-JUDGMENT
record planning_ai: assumption A-SC45-AI "AI does 20% of the target analysis for each target today and 75% at
    the end of AI progress, on the faster path. About 20,000 people already use one AI targeting system. In a
    demonstration, 20 soldiers with such a tool did the workload of a targeting cell of more than 2,000 in
    2003, and the imagery agency set the goal to automate 75% of the tasks of its imagery analysts. In a
    controlled test, soldiers with an AI tool got 18.2 points against 11.5 in the same time. These gains mix
    AI with new ways of work and policy, and cyber targets have no public equivalent, so the scenario takes
    less than the largest gain. It chooses values in the range 0.1 to 0.3 today and 0.6 to 0.9 at the end, and
    keeps the end at 0.9 or less so that a human still checks each target: observers warn that a fast review
    can become a formality."
    claims:
        E-SC45-AN-MAVEN-USERS
        E-SC45-WF-MAVEN-EXERCISE
        E-SC45-AN-NGA-75
        E-SC45-AN-BUNDESWEHR
        E-SC45-AN-IRAN-REVIEW
record access_stage: assumption A-SC45-COSTS "Access takes 0.3 expert-years for an attack and 1.2 for each
    target, and about half a year today. In a test range without defenders, an AI model took over a 32-step
    network that would take an expert about 20 hours, in 3 of 10 tries, and an automated framework made
    working exploits for about half of the recent known flaws that it tried, at about USD 3 each. At the end
    of AI progress AI does 87% of the work, and access takes days."
    claims:
        E-SC45-AISI-RANGE
        E-SC45-CVE-GENIE
record access_split: assumption A-SC45-COSTS "Initial access takes half of the time and of the work of access,
    and movement inside the network to the systems that the effect needs takes the other half. The scenario
    chooses the share."
record payload_stage: assumption A-SC45-COSTS "Building and testing the effect takes 1.6 expert-years for an
    attack and 0.4 for each target, and about 2.5 months today. A core team of five to ten people worked six
    months on one famous industrial payload, without its tests and management; a later one was built in as
    little as two months and was ready three weeks before its use. AI does 5% of the work today and 57% at the
    end, when it takes about 3.5 weeks."
    claims:
        E-SC45-STUXNET-TEAM
        E-SC45-SANDWORM-2022
record execution_stage: assumption A-SC45-COSTS "Running the attack takes 0.4 expert-years for an attack and
    0.1 for each target. One automated plan damaged devices in order in many networks at once. AI does 30% of
    the work today and 90% at the end."
    claims:
        E-SC45-POLAND-2025
record assessment_stage: assumption A-SC45-COSTS "Damage assessment takes 1 expert-year for an attack and 0.7
    for each target, and two assessments of one operation came 30 and 120 days after its start. AI does none
    of the work for the attack today and 30% at the end."
    claims:
        E-SC45-GLOWING-SYMPHONY
record assessment_ai: assumption A-SC45-AI "AI does 10% of the damage check for each target today and 60% at
    the end of AI progress, on the slower path. In kinetic war, an AI battle system has verified more than
    130,000 destroyed or damaged targets, and the US Air Force asks for automatic assessment at once after a
    strike, because the reports now come hours later. Cyber Command says that AI cut its analysis of network
    traffic from hours, days or weeks to minutes and hours. That is defensive work, and the effect of a cyber
    attack is harder to see than a hit target, so the scenario takes the slower path. It chooses values in the
    range 0.05 to 0.2 today and 0.5 to 0.7 at the end."
    claims:
        E-SC45-AN-DELTA
        E-SC45-AN-BDA-HOURS
        E-SC45-AN-CYBERCOM-TRAFFIC
        E-SC45-GLOWING-SYMPHONY
record lead_time: assumption A-SC45-COSTS "The time to build an attack is the sum of the times of initial
    access, of movement inside the network and of building and testing the effect: about 9 months today, and
    about a month at the end of AI progress. It is the mean time that a target spends in the three stocks
    before its attack is ready. In documented operations, first known access came 2 to 11 months before the
    effect."
    claims:
        E-SC45-POLAND-2025
record waiting_cost: assumption A-SC45-COSTS "An attack whose build takes longer than the warning needs access
    built in advance. Such an attack waits ready for its moment, needs upkeep and loses its access to patches
    and changes. The force builds lost access again while the attack waits: the model counts the work of that
    build and not its time. At rest, this work and the upkeep make the cost of waiting."
    claims:
        E-VOLT-PREPOSITION
record access_decay: assumption A-SC45-COSTS "Access built in advance is lost to patches and changes at 30% a
    year. A privately held zero-day lives 6.9 years on average before its disclosure, about 15% a year, and
    access also breaks when the target's network changes. The scenario chooses the rate."
    claims:
        E-SC45-ZERODAY-LIFE
record cost_learning: assumption A-SC45-COSTS "Lessons of attacks save part of the work of the stages that
    only attacks do: 30% when the force knows all it can learn. The access stage learns from the lessons of
    access instead. The scenario chooses the share."
record access_lessons: assumption A-SC45-LEARNING "The work of access learns from the accesses that attacks
    build and from the accesses that surveillance gains, as far as the two tasks overlap: 70%, the overlap of
    the tasks of two models of one aircraft. Each doubling of the lessons of access saves 10% of the work of
    access, half the 20% for each doubling of output in industry. The lessons of access fade at 20% a year: a
    privately held zero-day lives 6.9 years on average before its disclosure, about 15% a year, and staff
    leave. Surveillance's own work to build an access does not learn in the model. The scenario chooses the
    values."
    claims:
        E-SC45-BENKARD-LEARNING
        E-SC45-ZERODAY-LIFE
record exposure_basis: assumption A-SC45-COSTS "When the defenders expose an attack, it burns the capabilities
    that it holds; an attack that stays hidden keeps them for reuse. No source gives the chance of exposure.
    The scenario takes 25%, so that the force burns about 20 to 40 capabilities a year: a part of the 63 to
    106 zero-days that defenders catch in use worldwide each year, from all actors, none of them attributed to
    the United States."
    claims:
        E-SC45-ZD-2024-COUNT
        E-SC45-ZERODAY-2025
        E-SC45-ZD-2023-ACTORS
        E-SC45-ZD-2024-ACTORS
        E-SC45-ZD-2025-ACTORS
record known_flaws: assumption A-SC45-DEFENDERS "A known flaw works only on a target that has not fixed it by
    the time the force gets access: a quarter of targets never fix it, and the others fix it at 1.5 a year,
    the median of about six months for exploited flaws in large scans. So the faster AI builds access, the
    more targets are still open."
    claims:
        E-SC45-KEV-FIX
        E-SC45-PROXYLOGON-FIX
record tailoring: assumption A-SC45-AI "AI tailors the shared steps of an attack to each target, so its
    targets fall together less often: at the end of AI progress, AI tailors 80% of those steps. The scenario
    chooses the share."
record targets_basis: assumption A-SC45-RETURNS "One attack hits one target today. With AI, one attack hits
    about 30 targets at once. The scenario chooses both."
    claims:
        E-SC45-POLAND-2025
        E-SC45-AI-CAMPAIGN
record success_basis: assumption A-SC45-RETURNS "Today an attack gives its intended effect on a target one
    time in five. With every lesson learned, seven times in ten. The model moves between the two in log-odds,
    so the chance stays between 0 and 1."
    claims:
        V-C01
        E-SC45-STUXNET
        E-SC45-POLAND-2025
record value_scale: assumption A-SC45-RETURNS "By default the value of an effect is the value that today's
    split shows. The scale tests a country that values effects more or less than its split shows."
record diminishing_value: assumption A-SC45-RETURNS "The most valuable targets go first, so each effect is
    worth less as the targets hit in a year grow. The scenario chooses the scale."
record value_learning: assumption A-SC45-RETURNS "Lessons also raise the value of an effect: better targets
    and better timing. The value grows by the factor 1 + value_learning when every lesson is learned, and by
    that factor to the power of the gain on the way, so it stays positive when lessons fall below today's. The
    scenario chooses the gain."
record value_basis: assumption A-SC45-RETURNS "The value of a year's attacks is the value of an effect times
    the targets hit, with diminishing returns, the gain from lessons and, as an option, the gain from
    doctrine."
    claims:
        E-SC45-NOTPETYA-DAMAGE
record risk_value: assumption A-SC45-RETURNS "Leaders who are averse to risk count less of a spread value: the
    value divided by 1 plus half the aversion times the relative variance. By default the leaders count the
    expected value."
record attack_count: identity "The force starts attacks at the rate that the work of its operators allows: the
    operators on attacks over the operators' work of one attack. That work counts the cost of waiting. The
    analysts plan each attack after they assess the damage of the attacks made, so the force starts fewer when
    they cannot plan all of them."
record attack_pipeline: identity "An attack's targets move through stocks: initial access, access inside the
    network, and positioned on the systems that the effect needs. The effect then turns the targets of one
    attack into one attack. An attack built on demand is made when it is ready; an attack built in advance
    waits ready for its moment. Each step runs at the lower of its stock over its time and the pace that its
    hours allow, in one part for each source of the capabilities that it needs. So the mean time that a target
    spends in a stock is that stock's time while the hours suffice."
record assessment_time: assumption A-SC45-LEARNING "The damage assessment of an attack takes about four months
    today. One operation had a first assessment after 30 days, which could not yet assess the overall effect,
    and a second after 120 days. The lessons of an attack count when its assessment ends. At the end of AI
    progress the assessment takes half that time; the scenario chooses that value. When the analysts cannot do
    all of the work, the assessment takes longer."
    claims:
        E-SC45-GLOWING-SYMPHONY
record assessment_basis: assumption A-SC45-LEARNING "A damage assessment keeps part of what an outcome
    teaches: 40%, its chance to get an outcome right. In the 1991 air campaign the claimed hit rate of one
    bomber was 80% against 41% to 60% in a later audit, and the research notes put the reliability of two air
    campaigns' assessments near 0.3, so the range runs from 0.2 to 0.6. The scenario chooses the value."
    claims:
        E-SC45-CARTER-ISIS
        E-SC45-DESERT-STORM-BDA
record learning_basis: assumption A-SC45-LEARNING "The force learns from the assessed outcomes of its attacks
    when their assessment ends. The outcomes of the targets of one attack teach less than as many attacks,
    because they share causes."
record learning_curve: assumption A-SC45-LEARNING "Lessons get weight in the force's choices as they grow
    against a prior strength: with shape 1, the Bayesian weight of evidence of that strength. The scenario
    chooses 5,000 assessed outcomes, so today's lessons have about 9% of the weight and a few attacks teach
    little. With this strength today's split is a stable rest point, as two decades of a marginal effects
    program suggest. A weaker prior with a steeper curve can make it unstable: with a prior of about 500 or
    less and a shape above about 2, today is a tipping point between two regimes."
record learning_forms: assumption A-SC45-LEARNING "The exponential curve is the second form of the learning
    curve."
record lessons_today: assumption A-SC45-LEARNING "Today's lessons are a rest point: what today's attacks teach
    in a year over the rate at which lessons fade."
record forgetting: assumption A-SC45-LEARNING "The force loses its lessons of effects at 30% a year to
    patches, burned tools, staff turnover and secrecy that limits sharing. These are the skills that only
    attacks practice, so they fade faster than the lessons of access, which surveillance also practices. The
    scenario chooses the rate."
record burned_lessons: assumption A-SC45-LEARNING "As an option, a share of today's loss of lessons comes from
    exposed attacks that burn their tools, so that share grows with the attacks."
record lesson_value: identity "The value of a lesson is the change of the yearly net value of attacks with one
    more lesson, over the discount rate plus the rate at which lessons fade. Where the analysts cannot plan
    all of the attacks, one more operator adds fewer attacks, and lessons also save the analysts' work."
record learning_balance: identity "The lessons rise with the flow of assessed outcomes and fall with the flow
    of lessons lost."
record period_start: assumption A-SC45-STRUCTURE "The period starts in 2026. No attack starts it: the first
    move is the start of the period."
record harm_outside: assumption A-SC45-STRUCTURE "The model does not follow the harm of attacks to people or
    the economy."
record hazus_severity: convention HAZUS-SEVERITY "Serious casualties are the people who die and the people who
    survive with serious harm that lasts: the Hazus severity levels S4 and S3."
